COURSE_DATA["ins401"] = {"topics":[{"id":"t1","n":"Topic 1","title":"Introduction to Project Management","why":"The vocabulary for the whole course: project, PM, stakeholders, triple constraint, program vs portfolio, standards.","cards":[{"h":"What is a project?","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Definition — Project</b>A <b>temporary</b> endeavour (definite start and end) undertaken to create a <b>unique</b> product, service or result.</div>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Definition — Project management</b>The application of <b>knowledge, skills, tools and techniques</b> to project activities to meet the project requirements.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Project</th><th>Operations</th></tr><tr><td>Temporary, unique, time-bound</td><td>Ongoing, repetitive, business continuity</td></tr><tr><td>Building a new student portal</td><td>Running the portal every day</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Temporary does not mean short. A 5-year project is still temporary because it has an end.</div>"},{"h":"The project manager and stakeholders","b":"\n  <p><b>PM roles (lecturer):</b> liaison · team leadership · managing resources · communication · business objectives and benefits · managing constraints (scope, schedule, budget, quality) · managing risks.</p>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Definition — Stakeholder</b>A person, group or organisation that may <b>affect or be affected by</b> the project.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Internal</th><th>External</th></tr><tr><td>Sponsor, PMO, program manager, resource manager, PMs of other projects</td><td>Customers, end users, <b>shareholders</b>, regulatory bodies, suppliers</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trap\">On the lecturer's slides, shareholders are listed as <b>external</b>.</div>"},{"h":"Triple constraint · program vs portfolio","b":"\n  <p><b>Triple constraint:</b> scope, time, cost, with <b>quality in the centre</b>. Change one and the others move: add scope, and time and cost rise.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Program</th><th>Portfolio</th></tr><tr><td>A group of <b>related</b> projects managed together for benefits not available individually</td><td>Projects, programs <b>and operations</b> managed together to achieve <b>strategic objectives</b></td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Program = related. Portfolio = strategic (and can include operations).</div>"},{"h":"Standards, certifications and the PMI framework","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Key fact</th></tr><tr><td>PMI / PMBOK</td><td>USA; most comprehensive</td></tr><tr><td>PRINCE2</td><td>UK government origin; simpler, process-based</td></tr><tr><td>IPMA</td><td>Germany; competence-based</td></tr><tr><td>ISO 21500</td><td>International guidance standard</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Certifications:</b> CAPM → PMP → PgMP; PMI-ACP; PRINCE2 Foundation → Practitioner; MSP; IPMA levels D → A.</p>\n  <p><b>PMI framework:</b> 5 process groups (Initiating, Planning, Executing, Monitoring & Controlling, Closing) × 10 knowledge areas (Integration, Scope, Schedule, Cost, Quality, Resource, Communications, Risk, Procurement, Stakeholder).</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Knowledge areas: \"I Saw Six Cats Quietly Resting, Calmly Rubbing Paws Silently.\"</div>"},{"h":"Why project management matters","b":"\n  <p><b>Good PM gives:</b> clear objectives, efficient use of resources, risk control, on-time and on-budget delivery, stakeholder satisfaction, quality deliverables.</p>\n  <p><b>Poor PM causes:</b> missed deadlines, cost overruns, scope creep, poor quality and rework, unhappy stakeholders, failure (the lecturer cited abandoned projects in Nigeria).</p>\n  <p><b>Objective vs deliverable:</b> an objective is what the project aims to achieve (e.g. reduce registration time by 50%); a deliverable is a thing produced (the portal itself).</p>"},{"h":"Examples and scope of project management","b":"<p>Projects fulfil objectives by producing <b>deliverables</b>. Slide examples: website, mobile app, e-library, house, research, organisational change, business process improvement, wedding.</p><table><tr><th>Level</th><th>Examples</th></tr><tr><td>Individual</td><td>Wedding or birthday party, building a house, repairing a leaking roof</td></tr><tr><td>Group</td><td>Group vacation, community boreholes or streetlights, community sports complex</td></tr><tr><td>Organisational</td><td>ERP system, new mobile app, oil refinery, research for a new production process</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"A short history of project management","b":"<p>Practised for centuries, but it gained momentum as a concept in the <b>mid-20th century</b> through <b>construction, defence and aerospace</b>.</p><p>Rapid growth since the <b>1980s</b>: computers, analytical techniques and professional organisations.</p><p>It has evolved from <b>traditional</b> methods to include <b>agile</b> methods.</p>"},{"h":"Project failures in Nigeria (lecture examples)","b":"<ul><li><b>56%</b> of IT projects by Nigerian government institutions fail (NITDA, 2025).</li><li><b>Ajaokuta Steel Mill</b>: $10b over 43 years, still \"almost ready\".</li><li>Refineries and Ajaokuta: N6.5tr spent in 10 years without revival.</li><li>Abandoned national library: may cost N100 billion to revive.</li><li>Abuja <b>$460m CCTV</b> project: Senate-ordered probe.</li></ul><div class=\"hook\">Use one of these as your Scenario in any \"importance of PM\" essay.</div>"},{"h":"Business objectives","b":"<div class=\"def\"><b>Objective</b>Something toward which work is directed: a position to attain, a purpose, a result, a product or a service.</div><p>Business objectives are documented in the <b>Business Case</b>. Deliverables must align with them.</p><table><tr><th>Financial</th><th>Non-financial</th></tr><tr><td>Achieve a certain ROI</td><td>Quality metrics, throughput, speed, capacity, customer satisfaction, regulatory standards, contract terms</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Importance of PM: the slide lists","b":"<table><tr><th>Good PM gives</th><th>Poor PM leads to</th></tr><tr><td>Business objectives met</td><td>Objectives not achieved</td></tr><tr><td>Right products at the right time</td><td>Missed deadlines</td></tr><tr><td>Satisfied stakeholders</td><td>Unsatisfied stakeholders</td></tr><tr><td>Optimised resources</td><td>Cost overruns</td></tr><tr><td>Managed constraints</td><td>Poor quality, rework</td></tr><tr><td>Better change management; prompt response to risks</td><td><b>Loss of organisational reputation</b></td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Standards: the fine print","b":"<ul><li><b>PMI</b>: \"The Standard for Project Management\" is <b>Part II of the PMBOK Guide</b>; seen as the most robust and comprehensive.</li><li><b>PRINCE2</b> = <b>PR</b>ojects <b>IN C</b>ontrolled <b>E</b>nvironments. Rights held by <b>Axelos</b> (formerly the OGC). Simpler; certification needs less experience than PMI.</li><li><b>IPMA</b>: International Project Management Association; Germany; less widely used.</li><li><b>ISO 21500</b>: guidance on concepts and processes; less widely used.</li></ul><table><tr><th></th><th>PMI</th><th>PRINCE2</th><th>IPMA</th></tr><tr><td>Program</td><td>PgMP</td><td>MSP Practitioner</td><td>Level A</td></tr><tr><td>Project</td><td>PMP</td><td>PRINCE2 Practitioner</td><td>Levels B, C</td></tr><tr><td>Associate</td><td>CAPM</td><td>PRINCE2 Foundation</td><td>Level D</td></tr><tr><td>Agile</td><td>PMI-ACP</td><td>PRINCE2 Agile</td><td></td></tr></table>"}],"recall":[["Define a project.","A temporary endeavour undertaken to create a unique product, service or result."],["Define project management.","The application of knowledge, skills, tools and techniques to project activities to meet project requirements."],["Give two differences between a project and operations.","Projects are temporary and unique; operations are ongoing and repetitive (business continuity)."],["Define a stakeholder.","A person, group or organisation that may affect or be affected by the project."],["Are shareholders internal or external on the slides?","External."],["What sits at the centre of the triple constraint?","Quality (scope, time and cost are the sides)."],["Program vs portfolio?","Program: related projects for combined benefits. Portfolio: projects, programs and operations aligned to strategic objectives."],["Name the 5 PMI process groups.","Initiating, Planning, Executing, Monitoring & Controlling, Closing."],["Define a project manager (slides).","The person appointed by the organisation to lead the team responsible for achieving the project objectives."],["Give the three levels at which PM applies, with one example each.","Individual (wedding), group (community borehole), organisational (ERP system)."],["When did PM gain momentum as a concept, and through which industries?","Mid-20th century, through construction, defence and aerospace; rapid growth since the 1980s."],["What share of Nigerian government IT projects fail (NITDA)?","56%."],["Where are business objectives documented?","In the Business Case."],["Financial vs non-financial business objectives?","Financial: e.g. a target ROI. Non-financial: quality, throughput, speed, capacity, customer satisfaction, regulatory standards, contract terms."],["What does PRINCE2 stand for?","Projects IN Controlled Environments."],["Who holds the rights to PRINCE2?","Axelos Ltd (formerly the Office of Government Commerce, OGC)."],["Which IPMA level is the associate level?","Level D (Level A is program level)."]],"theory":[{"q":"Define a project and project management. Distinguish a project from operations, with examples.","pts":["Project definition: temporary + unique","PM definition: knowledge, skills, tools, techniques to meet requirements","Temporary vs ongoing","Unique vs repetitive","Example of each","Note: temporary ≠ short"],"model":"<p>A <b>project</b> is a temporary endeavour undertaken to create a unique product, service or result. <b>Project management</b> is the application of knowledge, skills, tools and techniques to project activities to meet the project requirements.</p><table><tr><th>Project</th><th>Operations</th></tr><tr><td>Temporary: definite start and end.</td><td>Ongoing with no planned end.</td></tr><tr><td>Unique output.</td><td>Repetitive output.</td></tr><tr><td>Ends when objectives are met.</td><td>Sustains the business (continuity).</td></tr><tr><td>E.g. building a hostel booking app.</td><td>E.g. the IT unit running the app daily.</td></tr></table><p>Temporary does not mean short: a five-year project is still temporary.</p>"},{"q":"Explain the triple constraint and distinguish a program from a portfolio.","pts":["Scope, time, cost named","Quality at the centre","Trade-off explained with example","Program definition","Portfolio definition (includes operations, strategic)","Clear contrast"],"model":"<p>The <b>triple constraint</b> links scope, time and cost, with quality at the centre. Changing one affects the others: adding features (scope) to a portal raises time and cost, or reduces quality if time and cost are fixed.</p><p>A <b>program</b> is a group of related projects managed in a coordinated way to obtain benefits not available from managing them individually (e.g. a university digitisation program: portal, e-library, payment system). A <b>portfolio</b> is a collection of projects, programs and operations managed together to achieve strategic objectives; the projects need not be related.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"A project is best described as…","o":["An ongoing, repetitive activity","A temporary endeavour to create a unique product, service or result","Any work done by a team","A group of related programs"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"A project lasting five years cannot be \"temporary\".","a":false,"w":"Temporary means it has a definite beginning and end, not that it is short."},{"k":"mcq","q":"On the lecturer's slides, shareholders are…","o":["Internal stakeholders","External stakeholders","Not stakeholders","Sponsors"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"A collection of projects, programs and operations managed to achieve strategic objectives is a…","o":["Program","Portfolio","PMO","Phase"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"PRINCE2 stands for Projects IN Controlled ___.","a":"environments"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which certification is PMI's entry-level (associate) credential?","o":["PMP","CAPM","PgMP","PMI-ACP"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which standard is competence-based and most used in Germany?","o":["PMI","PRINCE2","IPMA","ISO 21500"],"a":2},{"k":"gap","q":"Business objectives for a project are documented in the Business ___.","a":"case"},{"k":"tf","q":"Loss of organisational reputation is listed as a consequence of poor project management.","a":true},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is NOT one of the 10 PMI knowledge areas?","o":["Procurement","Stakeholder","Marketing","Integration"],"a":2},{"k":"gap","q":"Running a student portal every day is an example of ___, not a project.","a":"operations","alt":["operations management"]}]},{"id":"t2","n":"Topic 2","title":"The Project Management Life Cycle","why":"Phases, development approaches (predictive/adaptive/hybrid), the SDLC, and EEFs vs OPAs (a favourite trap).","cards":[{"h":"The five life-cycle phases","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Initiation</span><em>→</em><span>Planning</span><em>→</em><span>Execution</span><em>→</em><span>Monitoring & Control</span><em>→</em><span>Closing</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Key work</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Initiation</td><td>Authorise the project; project charter; appoint the PM; high-level scope</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Planning</td><td>Project management plan (scope, schedule, cost, risk…)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Execution</td><td>Produce deliverables; acquire and manage resources</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Monitoring & Control</td><td>Track against baselines; validate scope; runs <b>alongside</b> execution</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Closing</td><td>Final report; lessons learned; release the team; hand over to operations</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Predictive vs adaptive vs hybrid","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Predictive (waterfall)</th><th>Adaptive (agile)</th><th>Hybrid</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Scope, time and cost fixed <b>early</b>; formal change control</td><td>Work in <b>iterations</b>; scope detailed per iteration; product backlog; high change</td><td>Predictive for fixed parts, adaptive for evolving parts</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Building a hostel</td><td>A new mobile app</td><td>E-library build (fixed) + ERP software (evolving)</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"product backlog\", \"iterations\" → adaptive · \"fixed early\", \"formal change control\" → predictive</div>"},{"h":"The SDLC (seven phases)","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Planning</span><em>→</em><span>Analysis</span><em>→</em><span>Design</span><em>→</em><span>Development</span><em>→</em><span>Testing</span><em>→</em><span>Deployment</span><em>→</em><span>Maintenance</span></div>\n  <ul><li><b>Planning:</b> feasibility</li><li><b>Analysis:</b> detailed requirements</li><li><b>Design:</b> layouts, <b>database design</b></li><li><b>Development</b> (= implementation): coding</li><li><b>Testing:</b> alpha (against design specs) and beta (with users)</li><li><b>Deployment:</b> release, training</li><li><b>Maintenance:</b> fix and improve; may loop back</li></ul>\n  <div class=\"trap\">CA1 asked: \"creating databases\" happens in which SDLC phase? The trap is Design (database <i>design</i>) vs Development (actually <i>creating</i> it). Read the verb.</div>"},{"h":"EEFs vs OPAs","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Enterprise Environmental Factors (EEFs)</b>Conditions <b>not under the control</b> of the project team that influence the project. Internal: culture, geographic distribution, <b>existing IT systems</b>, existing staff expertise. External: regulations, market, socio-cultural, financial (exchange rates).</div>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Organisational Process Assets (OPAs)</b>Plans, processes, policies, procedures (templates, change-control procedures) and knowledge bases (lessons learned, historical files) of the organisation.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Existing IT systems and existing staff skills are <b>internal EEFs</b>, NOT OPAs. \"Existing systems and culture\" was a CA1 option.</div>"},{"h":"Initiation and planning: slide activities","b":"<p><b>Initiation</b> (authorises the project): goals, objectives and <b>success criteria</b> · identify stakeholders · high-level requirements and key deliverables · milestones and timelines · <b>pre-approved financial resources</b> · key risks · <b>project charter</b> · sign contracts (external customers) · appoint the PM.</p><p><b>Planning</b> (key output: <b>project management plan</b>): collect requirements · define scope · schedule · budget · resources · risks · <b>stakeholder engagement plan</b> · quality · <b>communications plan</b> · procurement plan.</p>"},{"h":"Execution, monitoring and closing: slide activities","b":"<p><b>Execution:</b> direct and manage project work · provide deliverables · provide <b>work performance information</b> · acquire resources · develop and manage the team · conduct procurements · manage risks, communications, quality and stakeholder engagement.</p><p><b>Monitoring and control:</b> <b>validate scope</b> (formal acceptance) · control scope, quality, cost and schedule against their <b>baselines</b> · track risk responses and identify new risks.</p><p><b>Closing</b> (project, phase or contract): confirm delivery and acceptance · <b>resolve all issues</b> · final report · lessons learned · release staff · <b>close project accounts</b> · transfer to operations.</p>"},{"h":"SDLC: what each phase does","b":"<p>SDLC = a structured framework (essentially a PM model) for building IS that meet user needs and are maintainable. It can be waterfall or agile.</p><table><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Slide activities</th></tr><tr><td>Planning</td><td>Objectives, scope, standards, resources, finance, stakeholders, risks, duration; <b>feasibility</b> and funding</td></tr><tr><td>Analysis</td><td>User needs; hardware, software, network specs; <b>requirements specification document</b></td></tr><tr><td>Design</td><td>Major components, UI layouts, database design, forms and reports, inputs/outputs, processing flow and logic</td></tr><tr><td>Development</td><td>Code, <b>system integration</b>, creating databases, <b>as-built documentation</b> and manuals</td></tr><tr><td>Testing</td><td>Module and integration tests; alpha (design specs); beta (users, user environment)</td></tr><tr><td>Deployment</td><td>Release to users; training</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Resolve issues; revise and improve; may return to phase 1</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"EEF and OPA examples in full","b":"<table><tr><th>Internal EEFs</th><th>External EEFs</th></tr><tr><td>Culture and governance (values, leadership style, hierarchy)</td><td>Government or industry standards (product, environment, labour, quality)</td></tr><tr><td>Geographic distribution (factories, virtual teams)</td><td>Marketplace (competitors, market share, brand)</td></tr><tr><td>Existing IT systems (scheduling software)</td><td>Socio-cultural (political climate, ethics, community)</td></tr><tr><td>Existing HR expertise and skills</td><td>Financial (exchange rates, interest, inflation, tariffs)</td></tr></table><p><b>OPAs</b>, two groups: (1) <b>processes, policies and procedures</b>: quality, procurement, health and safety policies; templates (PM plan, contract, risk analysis, stakeholder register); life cycles; change control. (2) <b>Knowledge bases</b>: lessons learned, financial data repositories (labour costs, budgets), files from previous projects.</p><div class=\"trap\">Slido question: \"An organisation's existing IT expertise is part of its OPAs.\" False: it is an internal EEF.</div>"}],"recall":[["Name the 5 project life-cycle phases.","Initiation, Planning, Execution, Monitoring & Control, Closing."],["What happens in Closing?","Final report, lessons learned, release the team, hand over to operations."],["Predictive vs adaptive life cycle?","Predictive: scope, time, cost fixed early with formal change control. Adaptive: iterations, scope defined per iteration, product backlog."],["When is a hybrid approach best?","When part of the project is fixed (predictive) and part is evolving (adaptive)."],["Name the 7 SDLC phases.","Planning, Analysis, Design, Development, Testing, Deployment, Maintenance."],["Alpha vs beta testing?","Alpha: against design specifications (internal). Beta: with real users."],["Define EEFs.","Conditions not under the control of the project team that influence the project (internal or external)."],["Are existing IT systems an EEF or OPA?","Internal EEF."],["Key output of the planning phase?","The project management plan."],["Name four initiation activities from the slides.","Goals and success criteria, identify stakeholders, high-level requirements, milestones, pre-approved funds, key risks, project charter, contracts, appoint the PM (any four)."],["What is validated in monitoring and control?","Scope: formal acceptance of completed deliverables."],["Name four closing activities.","Confirm delivery and acceptance, resolve issues, final report, lessons learned, release staff, close accounts, transfer to operations (any four)."],["Which SDLC phase produces the requirements specification document?","Analysis."],["Which SDLC phase includes system integration and as-built documentation?","Development (implementation)."],["Two OPA categories?","Processes, policies and procedures; organisational knowledge bases."],["Give two external EEFs.","Government/industry standards, marketplace conditions, socio-cultural conditions, financial considerations (any two)."]],"theory":[{"q":"Describe the five phases of the project management life cycle.","pts":["Initiation: charter, PM appointed","Planning: PM plan","Execution: deliverables, resources","M&C: track vs baselines, alongside execution","Closing: lessons learned, release team, handover","Logical order and brief example"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Initiation</b> — the project is authorised: business case reviewed, project charter issued, PM appointed, high-level scope set.</li><li><b>Planning</b> — the project management plan is developed: scope, schedule, cost, quality, risk, resources and communication.</li><li><b>Execution</b> — the team performs the work and produces deliverables; resources are acquired and managed.</li><li><b>Monitoring and controlling</b> — runs alongside execution: performance is tracked against baselines, changes are controlled, scope is validated.</li><li><b>Closing</b> — final report, lessons learned, team released, deliverables handed over to operations.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Distinguish between Enterprise Environmental Factors and Organisational Process Assets, giving examples of each.","pts":["EEF definition (not under team control)","Internal EEF examples","External EEF examples","OPA definition (processes/policies + knowledge bases)","OPA examples","Trap: existing IT/HR expertise = EEF"],"model":"<p><b>EEFs</b> are conditions not under the control of the project team that influence, constrain or direct the project. Internal: organisational culture, geographic distribution, existing IT systems, existing staff expertise. External: government regulations, market conditions, exchange rates, socio-cultural factors.</p><p><b>OPAs</b> are the organisation's own plans, processes, policies and procedures (templates, change-control procedures) and knowledge bases (lessons learned, historical project files).</p><p>Key distinction: an EEF is a <b>condition</b>; an OPA is a <b>document or record</b> the organisation owns. Existing IT systems are therefore an internal EEF, not an OPA.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"Which phase comes right after planning?","o":["Initiation","Execution","Monitoring and control","Closing"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which SDLC phase focuses most on gathering detailed user and system requirements?","o":["Planning","Analysis","Design","Testing"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"An organisation's existing IT expertise is part of its organisational process assets.","a":false,"w":"Existing staff expertise is an internal EEF."},{"k":"mcq","q":"\"Creating databases\" is listed under which SDLC phase?","o":["Analysis","Design","Development","Deployment"],"a":2},{"k":"mcq","q":"Developing the project charter happens in…","o":["Initiation","Planning","Execution","Closing"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"Scope, cost and time fixed early with formal change control describes…","o":["Adaptive","Predictive","Hybrid","Iterative"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"In an adaptive life cycle, each iteration addresses part of the work in the product ___.","a":"backlog"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Exchange rates and inflation are…","o":["Internal EEFs","External EEFs","OPAs","Deliverables"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"A lessons-learned database is…","o":["An internal EEF","An external EEF","An OPA (knowledge base)","A baseline"],"a":2},{"k":"tf","q":"Beta testing checks the system against design specifications.","a":false,"w":"Alpha tests against design specs; beta tests with users in the user environment."},{"k":"gap","q":"Closing activities include closure of project ___.","a":"accounts"},{"k":"mcq","q":"User training happens in which SDLC phase?","o":["Testing","Deployment","Maintenance","Design"],"a":1}]},{"id":"t3","n":"Topic 3","title":"Tools, Techniques and Processes","why":"ITTOs, the five process groups and their exceptions, tool vs technique, and the classic estimating techniques.","cards":[{"h":"Processes and ITTOs","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Process</b>A set of activities that turns <b>inputs</b> into <b>outputs</b> using <b>tools and techniques</b> (ITTO). An output becomes an input to another process or a deliverable.</div>\n  <p><b>Process groups:</b> Initiating (2 processes: <b>Develop Project Charter</b>, <b>Identify Stakeholders</b>) · Planning · Executing · Monitoring & Controlling · Closing (1: <b>Close Project or Phase</b>).</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Exceptions to the \"verb rule\": <b>Identify Stakeholders = Initiating</b>; Identify Risks = Planning; <b>Develop Team, Manage Quality, Acquire Resources = Executing</b>; Validate Scope, Perform Integrated Change Control = M&C. Scope, Schedule and Cost have <b>no</b> executing processes.</div>"},{"h":"Tool vs technique + key definitions","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Technique</th></tr><tr><td>Something <b>tangible</b>: template, software</td><td>A systematic <b>procedure</b> carried out by a person; may use tools</td></tr><tr><td>MS Project, a checklist template</td><td>Expert judgement, data gathering, inspection</td></tr></table>\n  <ul><li><b>Analogous estimating:</b> uses a similar past project</li><li><b>Parametric:</b> algorithm + parameters (cost per line of code)</li><li><b>Decomposition:</b> break into smaller parts</li><li><b>Prototypes:</b> working model for early feedback</li><li><b>Trend analysis:</b> forecast from past performance · <b>Variance analysis:</b> baseline vs actual · <b>Root cause analysis:</b> underlying reason</li><li><b>Earned value analysis:</b> combines scope, schedule and cost</li><li><b>Inspection:</b> examine whether work conforms to standards</li></ul>\n  <div class=\"trap\">CA1 asked which is NOT a technique: <b>scheduling software</b> (a tool).</div>"},{"h":"Work performance data → information → reports","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Data</span><em>(Direct & Manage Project Work)</em><em>→</em><span>Information</span><em>(controlling processes)</em><em>→</em><span>Reports</span><em>(Monitor & Control Project Work)</em></div>\n  <p><b>Direct and Manage Project Work</b> outputs: deliverables, work performance data, issue log, change requests. (CA1: the assumption log is NOT an output.)</p>\n  <p><b>PM software:</b> MS Project (enterprise), Asana (multiple views), ClickUp (sprints, flexible views), Trello (boards, lists, cards — Kanban), Jira (software teams, agile/scrum).</p>"},{"h":"Process and process group: definitions","b":"<div class=\"def\"><b>PM processes</b>A series of PM activities executed while managing the project life cycle. Each produces one or more outputs from one or more inputs using tools and techniques.</div><div class=\"def\"><b>Process group</b>A logical grouping of PM processes to achieve specific project objectives. PMBOK has five.</div><p>Initiating = obtain <b>authorisation</b> to start a project or phase. Planning = establish scope, refine objectives, define the course of action. Executing = complete the work in the plan. M&C = track, review, regulate, and initiate changes. Closing = formally close the project, phase or contract.</p>"},{"h":"Processes in each group (slide lists)","b":"<p><b>Planning:</b> develop PM plan · collect requirements · define scope · create WBS · define activities · develop schedule · determine budget · plan resource management · identify risks · plan risk responses · plan stakeholder engagement · plan quality · plan communications · plan procurements.</p><p><b>Executing:</b> direct and manage project work · <b>manage project knowledge</b> · manage quality · acquire resources · develop team · manage team · conduct procurements · <b>implement risk responses</b> · manage communications · manage stakeholder engagement.</p><p><b>M&C:</b> monitor and control project work · perform integrated change control · validate scope · control scope, schedule, quality, resources, costs, procurements · monitor communications, risks, stakeholder engagement.</p><div class=\"trap\">Slido: \"Identify stakeholders is a planning process.\" False: it is Initiating.</div>"},{"h":"More techniques from the slides","b":"<table><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>Expert judgement</td><td>Judgement based on expertise in an area, discipline or industry</td></tr><tr><td>Data gathering</td><td>Collect data, e.g. <b>brainstorming, focus groups, interviews</b></td></tr><tr><td>Decision making</td><td>Choose among alternatives, e.g. <b>voting</b></td></tr><tr><td>Critical path method</td><td>Minimum duration and schedule flexibility</td></tr><tr><td>Decision tree analysis</td><td>Evaluate a chain of options under uncertainty</td></tr><tr><td>Resource optimisation</td><td>Adjust dates to balance resource demand and supply</td></tr><tr><td>Interpersonal and team skills</td><td>Build and keep relationships</td></tr></table><div class=\"trap\">Slido: \"Interview is a tool.\" False: interviewing is a data-gathering <b>technique</b>.</div>"},{"h":"PMIS","b":"<div class=\"def\"><b>Project management information system (PMIS)</b>An information system of tools and techniques used to gather, integrate and disseminate the outputs of PM processes.</div><p>Examples: scheduling software, work authorisation systems, configuration management systems, interfaces to corporate knowledge repositories.</p><p>Conclusion slide: tools and techniques should be <b>tailored</b> to each process.</p>"},{"h":"ITTO examples: charter and PM plan","b":"<table><tr><th></th><th>Develop Project Charter (Initiating)</th><th>Develop PM Plan (Planning)</th></tr><tr><td>Inputs</td><td>Business documents (<b>business case</b>), agreements, EEFs, OPAs</td><td><b>Project charter</b>, outputs of other processes, EEFs, OPAs</td></tr><tr><td>T&T</td><td>Expert judgement, data gathering, interpersonal skills (conflict management, facilitation, meeting management), meetings</td><td>Same, plus <b>checklists</b></td></tr><tr><td>Outputs</td><td><b>Project charter</b>, <b>assumption log</b></td><td><b>Project management plan</b></td></tr></table>"},{"h":"ITTO examples: M&C and closing","b":"<p><b>Monitor and Control Project Work</b>: input = work performance <b>information</b>; T&T = data analysis (alternative, cost-benefit, earned value, root cause, trend, variance), decision making (voting); outputs = <b>work performance reports</b>, change requests, plan and document updates.</p><p><b>Close Project or Phase</b>: inputs include the charter, <b>accepted deliverables</b>, agreements and procurement documents; outputs = lessons learned register update, <b>final product, service or result transition</b>, <b>final report</b>, OPA updates.</p>"}],"recall":[["What does ITTO stand for?","Inputs, Tools & Techniques, Outputs."],["Which 2 processes are in the Initiating group?","Develop Project Charter and Identify Stakeholders."],["Which process group has only one process?","Closing (Close Project or Phase)."],["Identify Stakeholders belongs to which process group?","Initiating."],["Tool vs technique?","A tool is tangible (template, software); a technique is a systematic procedure performed by a person."],["Analogous vs parametric estimating?","Analogous uses a similar past project; parametric uses an algorithm with parameters."],["Data → information → reports: which process produces work performance data?","Direct and Manage Project Work."],["Which PM tool uses boards, lists and cards (Kanban)?","Trello."],["Define a process group.","A logical grouping of PM processes to achieve specific project objectives."],["Which group's processes obtain authorisation to start a project or phase?","Initiating."],["Name three executing processes besides Direct and Manage Project Work.","Manage project knowledge, manage quality, acquire resources, develop team, manage team, conduct procurements, implement risk responses, manage communications, manage stakeholder engagement (any three)."],["Two outputs of Develop Project Charter.","Project charter and assumption log."],["Main input of Develop Project Management Plan.","The project charter."],["What does decision tree analysis evaluate?","The implications of a chain of multiple options under uncertainty."],["What is resource optimisation?","Adjusting activity start and finish dates to balance resource demand with available supply."],["Define PMIS and give two examples.","An information system of tools and techniques that gathers, integrates and disseminates PM process outputs; e.g. scheduling software, work authorisation systems, configuration management systems."],["Main output of Monitor and Control Project Work?","Work performance reports (plus change requests)."],["Name two outputs of Close Project or Phase.","Final report, final product/service/result transition, lessons learned update, OPA updates (any two)."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the five process groups and identify the exceptions students commonly get wrong.","pts":["Five groups named and described","Initiating: charter + identify stakeholders","Closing: one process","Identify Stakeholders = Initiating","Develop Team / Manage Quality / Acquire Resources = Executing","Validate Scope = M&C"],"model":"<p>PMI organises processes into five groups: <b>Initiating</b> (define and authorise: Develop Project Charter, Identify Stakeholders), <b>Planning</b> (establish scope, objectives and the plan), <b>Executing</b> (complete the work), <b>Monitoring and Controlling</b> (track, review and regulate performance) and <b>Closing</b> (Close Project or Phase — the only process).</p><p>Common exceptions: Identify Stakeholders is Initiating even though \"identify\" sounds like planning; Identify Risks is Planning; Develop Team, Manage Quality and Acquire Resources are Executing; Validate Scope and Perform Integrated Change Control are Monitoring and Controlling.</p>"},{"q":"Distinguish between a tool and a technique, and explain four techniques used in project management.","pts":["Tool definition (tangible)","Technique definition (procedure)","Technique 1 explained","Technique 2 explained","Technique 3 explained","Technique 4 explained"],"model":"<p>A <b>tool</b> is something tangible, such as a template or software program, used to produce an output. A <b>technique</b> is a defined, systematic procedure carried out by a person, which may use one or more tools.</p><ol><li><b>Expert judgement</b> — using people with specialised knowledge.</li><li><b>Analogous estimating</b> — estimating from a similar past project.</li><li><b>Parametric estimating</b> — using an algorithm and parameters (e.g. cost per square metre).</li><li><b>Variance analysis</b> — comparing baseline to actual performance to find deviations.</li></ol>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"tf","q":"\"Identify stakeholders\" is a process within the planning process group.","a":false,"w":"It is in the Initiating group."},{"k":"tf","q":"An interview is a tool that can be used for data gathering.","a":false,"w":"Interviewing is a technique; tools are tangible things like templates or software."},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a tool rather than a technique?","o":["Expert judgement","Scheduling software","Inspection","Root cause analysis"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is NOT an output of Direct and Manage Project Work?","o":["Deliverables","Work performance data","Issue log","Assumption log"],"a":3},{"k":"mcq","q":"The outputs of Develop Project Charter are the project charter and the…","o":["Issue log","Assumption log","Risk register","WBS"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which process produces work performance reports?","o":["Direct and Manage Project Work","Monitor and Control Project Work","Close Project or Phase","Develop Project Charter"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Estimating cost from historical data of a similar project is ___ estimating.","a":"analogous"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Determining the cause and degree of difference between baseline and actual is…","o":["Trend analysis","Variance analysis","Earned value analysis","Decomposition"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which group has only one process?","o":["Initiating","Planning","Closing","Executing"],"a":2},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which PM tool uses boards, lists and cards based on Kanban?","o":["Jira","Trello","MS Project","Asana"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"\"Implement risk responses\" belongs to which group?","o":["Planning","Executing","Monitoring and Controlling","Closing"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Dividing scope and deliverables into smaller, manageable parts is called ___.","a":"decomposition"}]},{"id":"t4","n":"Topic 4","title":"Managing Project Teams I","why":"Resource processes, motivation, Goleman's six leadership styles and Tuckman's five stages.","cards":[{"h":"The three team processes","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Group</th><th>Key outputs</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Plan Resource Management</td><td>Planning</td><td>Resource management plan + <b>team charter</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Estimate Activity Resources</td><td>Planning</td><td>Resource requirements + <b>resource breakdown structure (RBS)</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Acquire Resources</td><td>Executing</td><td>Project team assignments + <b>resource calendars</b> (also an input)</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Team charter</b> = team values, agreements and operating guidelines. Different from the <b>project charter</b> (authorises the project).</p>\n  <p><b>Resource breakdown structure</b> = hierarchy of resources by <b>category and type</b> (labour, material, equipment, supplies).</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">CA1: the team charter is an output of <b>Plan Resource Management</b>.</div>"},{"h":"Motivating team members","b":"\n  <p>Ten strategies: open communication · clear goals and roles · timely conflict management · build trust · team-building · lead by example · collaborative decisions · celebrate small wins · training and tools · <b>tailored</b> recognition and rewards.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Tailored = different people are motivated by different rewards.</div>"},{"h":"Goleman's six leadership styles","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Style</th><th>Phrase</th><th>When</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Commanding</td><td>\"Do what I tell you\"</td><td>Crisis; use sparingly</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Visionary</td><td>\"Come with me\"</td><td>New vision / direction needed</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Affiliative</td><td>\"People come first\"</td><td>Heal rifts, motivate in stress</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Democratic</td><td>\"What do you think?\"</td><td>Build buy-in; slow, not for urgency</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Pacesetting</td><td>\"Do as I do, now\"</td><td>Quick results from a competent team; use sparingly</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Coaching</td><td>\"Try this\"</td><td>Long-term development; time-consuming</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Tuckman's five stages","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Forming</span><em>→</em><span>Storming</span><em>→</em><span>Norming</span><em>→</em><span>Performing</span><em>→</em><span>Adjourning</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Best style</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Forming</td><td>Get acquainted; ground rules</td><td>Visionary + coaching</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Storming</td><td>Conflict; members challenge roles</td><td>Affiliative + commanding</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Norming</td><td>Norms agreed; cohesion</td><td>Democratic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Performing</td><td>Optimal output</td><td>Democratic</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Adjourning</td><td>Team disbands</td><td>—</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Three team processes: definitions","b":"<ul><li><b>Plan Resource Management</b>: defines how to estimate, acquire, manage and use team and physical resources.</li><li><b>Estimate Activity Resources</b>: estimates team resources and the type and quantity of material, equipment and supplies.</li><li><b>Acquire Resources</b>: obtains team members, facilities, equipment, materials and supplies.</li></ul><div class=\"def\"><b>Resource management plan</b>A component of the PM plan describing how resources are acquired, allocated, monitored and controlled.</div>"},{"h":"What goes in a team charter","b":"<p>The team charter documents the team's <b>operating guidelines</b>. It may include:</p><ol><li>Team values</li><li>Communication guidelines</li><li>Decision-making criteria and process</li><li>Conflict resolution process</li><li>Meeting guidelines</li><li>Team agreements</li></ol><div class=\"hook\">V-C-D-C-M-A: Values, Communication, Decisions, Conflict, Meetings, Agreements.</div>"},{"h":"Team process tools and outputs","b":"<table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Key T&T</th><th>Outputs</th></tr><tr><td>Plan Resource Mgmt</td><td>Hierarchical charts, <b>responsibility assignment matrix</b>, text-oriented formats, organisational theory</td><td>Resource mgmt plan, team charter</td></tr><tr><td>Estimate Activity Resources</td><td><b>Bottom-up</b>, analogous, parametric estimating; alternative analysis; PMIS</td><td>Resource requirements, <b>basis of estimates</b>, RBS</td></tr><tr><td>Acquire Resources</td><td>Multicriteria decision analysis, <b>negotiation</b>, <b>pre-assignment</b>, <b>virtual teams</b></td><td>Physical resource assignments, project team assignments, resource calendars, change requests</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Resource requirements, team assignments, calendars","b":"<p><b>Resource requirements</b>: types and quantities of resources for each work package or activity; can be added up per work package, WBS branch and whole project.</p><p><b>Project team assignments</b>: documents the team, roles and responsibilities (team directory, names in org charts and schedules).</p><p><b>Resource calendar</b>: working days and shifts when each resource is available. Consider <b>time zones</b> and expertise.</p><div class=\"trap\">Slido: resource calendar is both an input and an output of Acquire Resources. True.</div>"},{"h":"Motivation strategies explained","b":"<table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Why it works (slides)</th></tr><tr><td>Open communication</td><td>Everyone can speak up and give feedback</td></tr><tr><td>Clear goals and roles</td><td>Removes ambiguity, minimises conflict</td></tr><tr><td>Timely conflict management</td><td>Keeps the team focused</td></tr><tr><td>Build trust</td><td>Everyone respected and trusted</td></tr><tr><td>Lead by example</td><td>PM \"walks the talk\"</td></tr><tr><td>Collaborative decisions</td><td>Members feel important; ownership</td></tr><tr><td>Celebrate small wins</td><td>Boosts confidence</td></tr><tr><td>Training and tools</td><td>Confidence and performance</td></tr><tr><td>Tailored rewards</td><td>More roles, challenges, gifts, praise</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Goleman: competences and weaknesses","b":"<table><tr><th>Style</th><th>EI competences</th><th>Weakness</th></tr><tr><td>Commanding</td><td>Drive, initiative, self-control</td><td>Stifles creativity, dependency</td></tr><tr><td>Visionary</td><td>Self-confidence, empathy, change catalyst</td><td>Other good ideas neglected</td></tr><tr><td>Affiliative</td><td>Empathy, relationships, communication</td><td>Low performance, mediocrity</td></tr><tr><td>Democratic</td><td>Collaboration, team leadership, communication</td><td>Slow decisions</td></tr><tr><td>Pacesetting</td><td>Conscientiousness, drive, initiative</td><td>Stress and <b>burnout</b></td></tr><tr><td>Coaching</td><td>Developing others, empathy, self-awareness</td><td>Time-consuming</td></tr></table><p>Commanding also suits a <b>turnaround</b> or <b>problem employees</b>. No style fits every situation: adapt to the team, the project and the culture.</p>"}],"recall":[["What are the 2 key outputs of Plan Resource Management?","Resource management plan and team charter."],["What does the resource breakdown structure show?","Resources in a hierarchy by category and type."],["Which process group is Acquire Resources in?","Executing."],["Which Goleman style says \"Do as I do, now\"?","Pacesetting."],["Which style heals rifts (\"people come first\")?","Affiliative."],["Name Tuckman's five stages.","Forming, Storming, Norming, Performing, Adjourning."],["In which stage do members challenge roles?","Storming."],["Team charter vs project charter?","Team charter: team values and operating guidelines. Project charter: formally authorises the project."],["Define the resource management plan.","A component of the PM plan describing how project resources are acquired, allocated, monitored and controlled."],["Name four items in a team charter.","Team values, communication guidelines, decision-making process, conflict resolution process, meeting guidelines, team agreements (any four)."],["Name the four T&T of Acquire Resources.","Multicriteria decision analysis, negotiation, pre-assignment, virtual teams."],["Name three outputs of Estimate Activity Resources.","Resource requirements, basis of estimates, resource breakdown structure."],["Define a resource calendar.","A calendar showing the working days and shifts when each resource is available."],["Is the resource calendar an input or output of Acquire Resources?","Both."],["Weakness of the pacesetting style?","High stress and burnout; use sparingly."],["Weakness of the affiliative style?","Too much focus on relationships can lead to low performance and mediocrity."],["Weakness of the visionary style?","Over-reliance on the leader's vision can neglect other good ideas."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain Tuckman's five stages of team development and the leadership style suitable for each.","pts":["Forming + style","Storming + style","Norming + style","Performing + style","Adjourning","Practical example"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Forming</b> — members meet and learn about the project; ground rules set. Visionary and coaching styles give direction.</li><li><b>Storming</b> — conflict as members compete and challenge roles. Affiliative style heals rifts; commanding if a crisis develops.</li><li><b>Norming</b> — norms agreed, trust builds. Democratic style invites participation.</li><li><b>Performing</b> — the team works at its best. Democratic style lets the team lead itself.</li><li><b>Adjourning</b> — work is complete and the team disbands; recognise contributions.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Describe Goleman's six leadership styles and when each is appropriate.","pts":["Commanding","Visionary","Affiliative","Democratic","Pacesetting","Coaching","\"Use sparingly\" for commanding and pacesetting"],"model":"<ul><li><b>Commanding</b> (\"Do what I tell you\") — crises; use sparingly.</li><li><b>Visionary</b> (\"Come with me\") — when a new vision or direction is needed.</li><li><b>Affiliative</b> (\"People come first\") — to heal rifts and motivate under stress.</li><li><b>Democratic</b> (\"What do you think?\") — to build buy-in; not ideal in urgent situations.</li><li><b>Pacesetting</b> (\"Do as I do, now\") — fast results from a highly competent team; use sparingly.</li><li><b>Coaching</b> (\"Try this\") — long-term development of people; time-consuming.</li></ul>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"The team charter is an output of…","o":["Acquire Resources","Plan Resource Management","Estimate Activity Resources","Develop Team"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"The resource calendar is both an input and an output of Acquire Resources.","a":true},{"k":"mcq","q":"A hierarchy of resources by category and type is the…","o":["WBS","RBS","RAM","OBS"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Acquire Resources is in which process group?","o":["Planning","Executing","Monitoring and Controlling","Initiating"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a tool/technique of Acquire Resources?","o":["Pre-assignment","Decomposition","Bottom-up estimating","Inspection"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"\"Do what I tell you\" is which Goleman style?","o":["Pacesetting","Commanding","Coaching","Visionary"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Best style to build buy-in and consensus?","o":["Democratic","Commanding","Pacesetting","Affiliative"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which style risks burnout?","o":["Coaching","Pacesetting","Affiliative","Democratic"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"In which Tuckman stage do members challenge each other's roles?","o":["Forming","Storming","Norming","Adjourning"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Tuckman's final stage, when the team disbands, is ___.","a":"adjourning"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which styles suit the forming stage?","o":["Visionary and coaching","Affiliative and commanding","Democratic","Pacesetting"],"a":0},{"k":"gap","q":"A responsibility ___ matrix is a data representation technique in Plan Resource Management.","a":"assignment"}]},{"id":"t5","n":"Topic 5","title":"Managing Project Teams II","why":"Power (French & Raven), conflict sources and strategies, virtual teams, communications management.","cards":[{"h":"Six sources of power","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Power</th><th>Based on</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Legitimate</td><td>Position or rank</td></tr><tr><td>Reward</td><td>Ability to give rewards</td></tr><tr><td>Coercive</td><td>Threat of punishment</td></tr><tr><td>Expert</td><td>Expertise / knowledge</td></tr><tr><td>Referent</td><td>Admiration, personal connection</td></tr><tr><td>Informational</td><td>Controlling access to information</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Expert = what you know. Informational = what information you control.</div>"},{"h":"Conflict: sources and five strategies","b":"\n  <p>Conflict is <b>inevitable</b> and not inherently good or bad. <b>Sources:</b> scarce resources · scheduling priorities · work styles · role/task ambiguity · poor communication · personality and values.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Avoiding</td><td>Withdraw or postpone</td></tr><tr><td>Accommodating</td><td>Concede to the other side</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Compromising</td><td>Middle ground; <b>lose-lose</b> on the slides</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Forcing / competing</td><td>Use power; suits emergencies</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Collaborating</td><td>Problem-solve; <b>win-win</b>, fully satisfies everyone</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Global and virtual teams","b":"\n  <p><b>Virtual team</b> = shared goal, little or no face-to-face contact (can still be local). <b>Global</b> = spread across countries.</p>\n  <p><b>Benefits:</b> wider talent pool, expertise, home-based workers, shifts across time zones, inclusion of people with disabilities, no travel or office costs.</p>\n  <p><b>Challenges:</b> language, time zones, tracking work, culture, knowledge gaps, labour laws.</p>\n  <p><b>Solutions:</b> clear channels, clear goals, flexible schedules, inclusivity, technology tools, regular check-ins.</p>"},{"h":"Communications management","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Key output</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Plan Communications Management</td><td>Communications management plan</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Manage Communications</td><td>Project communications (collect, create, distribute, store, retrieve)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Monitor Communications</td><td>Work performance information + change requests</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Enhancing communication:</b> active listening · writing style · meeting management · presentations · open communication and feedback · appropriate media.</p>"},{"h":"Power and leadership: definitions","b":"<div class=\"def\"><b>Power</b>\"The ability to direct or influence the behaviour of others or the course of events.\"</div><div class=\"def\"><b>Leadership</b>\"The ability to influence, guide and motivate a group of people toward a common goal or vision.\"</div><p>The six sources come from the <b>French and Raven</b> bases of power model. How a PM uses power affects the team's success.</p>"},{"h":"Conflict: definition and the two axes","b":"<div class=\"def\"><b>Conflict</b>A serious disagreement or argument between two or more individuals or groups.</div><p>Inevitable because people's perspectives and desires differ. Handled well: creative solutions, personal growth, stronger relationships. Handled badly: hostility, violence, destruction.</p><p>The strategy chart has two axes: <b>concern for self (assertiveness)</b> and <b>concern for others (cooperativeness)</b>. Collaborating is high on both; avoiding is low on both; forcing is high self, low others; accommodating is low self, high others.</p><div class=\"trap\">The axis placement of each strategy is the standard reading of the chart, not text on the slide.</div>"},{"h":"Why global teams form","b":"<ul><li>Companies with offices in several countries need integrated corporate projects.</li><li>Expertise is not available locally.</li></ul><p>Global team members are geographically, linguistically and culturally diverse. Virtual teams open new options for acquiring members for <b>global or local</b> projects.</p><p>Extra benefit on the slides: projects that would be held or cancelled because of <b>travel costs</b> can go ahead.</p>"},{"h":"Communications plan: what it contains","b":"<p>Communications management ensures the <b>information needs</b> of the project and stakeholders are met.</p><p>The <b>communications management plan</b> says how communication is planned, structured, implemented and monitored. Components:</p><ol><li>Stakeholder communication requirements</li><li>Information: language, format, content, detail</li><li><b>Escalation processes</b></li><li>Reason for distribution</li><li>Timeframe and frequency</li><li>Person responsible</li><li>Recipients and their needs</li><li>Methods and technologies (memos, email, press releases, social media)</li><li>Resources: time and budget</li></ol>"},{"h":"Communications tools and outputs","b":"<p><b>Plan</b> T&T: communication requirements analysis, communication technology, communication methods, <b>stakeholder engagement assessment matrix</b>, meetings.</p><p><b>Manage</b> T&T: communication skills (competence, feedback, nonverbal, presentations), PMIS, <b>project reporting</b>, interpersonal skills (active listening, conflict management, meeting management, networking, cultural awareness).</p><p><b>Project communications</b> (artifacts): performance reports, deliverable status, schedule progress, cost incurred, presentations.</p><p><b>Work performance information</b> (Monitor): compares communications implemented with those planned, plus feedback such as surveys on effectiveness.</p>"},{"h":"Communication methods and good practice","b":"<p><b>Methods:</b> notice boards · newsletters · letters to staff · press releases · presentations · email and intranet · phone · team briefings · face-to-face meetings · focus groups · instant messaging and social media · video conferencing · web portals and knowledge repositories.</p><p><b>Enhancing communication:</b> <b>active listening</b> (acknowledge, clarify, confirm, remove barriers) · clear, concise <b>writing</b> (active vs passive voice) · <b>meeting management</b> (agenda, essential invitees, handle conflict) · <b>presentations</b> (body language, visual aids) · open feedback · the right <b>medium</b>.</p>"}],"recall":[["Name French and Raven's six power sources (as on the slides).","Legitimate, reward, coercive, expert, referent, informational."],["Power based on admiration?","Referent."],["Name 4 sources of conflict.","Any four: scarce resources, scheduling priorities, work styles, role ambiguity, poor communication, personality/values."],["Which strategy is win-win?","Collaborating."],["Which strategy is lose-lose on the slides?","Compromising."],["Avoiding vs accommodating?","Avoiding withdraws or postpones; accommodating concedes to the other party."],["Name the 3 communications processes.","Plan Communications Management, Manage Communications, Monitor Communications."],["Can a virtual team be local?","Yes. Virtual describes how they work (little face-to-face), not where."],["Define power (slides).","The ability to direct or influence the behaviour of others or the course of events."],["Define leadership (slides).","The ability to influence, guide and motivate a group of people toward a common goal or vision."],["Define conflict.","A serious disagreement or argument between two or more individuals or groups."],["Two possible outcomes of well-managed conflict.","Creative solutions, personal growth, stronger relationships (any two)."],["What are the two axes of the conflict strategies chart?","Concern for self (assertiveness) and concern for others (cooperativeness)."],["Two reasons global teams form.","Offices in different countries needing integrated projects; expertise not available locally."],["Name four components of the communications management plan.","Stakeholder requirements, information to communicate, escalation processes, reason, timeframe and frequency, person responsible, recipients, methods, resources (any four)."],["What does work performance information from Monitor Communications compare?","Communications implemented against those planned, plus feedback on effectiveness."],["Define active listening.","Acknowledging, clarifying and confirming, understanding, and removing barriers that hurt comprehension."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain five strategies for managing conflict in project teams, stating when each is suitable.","pts":["Avoiding","Accommodating","Compromising (lose-lose)","Forcing (emergencies)","Collaborating (win-win)","Conflict is not inherently bad"],"model":"<p>Conflict is inevitable in teams and is not inherently good or bad; it must be managed.</p><ol><li><b>Avoiding</b> — withdrawing or postponing; suitable when the issue is trivial or tempers need to cool.</li><li><b>Accommodating</b> — conceding to the other party; suitable when harmony matters more than the issue.</li><li><b>Compromising</b> — each side gives up something (lose-lose on the slides); suitable when time is short.</li><li><b>Forcing/competing</b> — using power to impose a solution; suitable in emergencies.</li><li><b>Collaborating</b> — working together for a solution that fully satisfies everyone (win-win); best when time allows and commitment matters.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Discuss the benefits and challenges of virtual teams, and suggest ways to make them effective.","pts":["Definition","3+ benefits","3+ challenges","3+ solutions","Example"],"model":"<p>A <b>virtual team</b> shares a goal but has little or no face-to-face contact.</p><p><b>Benefits:</b> access to a wider talent pool and expertise; home-based workers; work across time zones in shifts; inclusion of people with disabilities; lower travel and office costs.</p><p><b>Challenges:</b> language barriers, time-zone differences, difficulty tracking work, cultural differences, knowledge gaps and differing labour laws.</p><p><b>Solutions:</b> clear communication channels, clear goals, flexible schedules, inclusive practices, collaboration tools (Jira, Slack, Teams) and regular check-ins.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"Power derived from controlling access to information others need is…","o":["Expert","Referent","Informational","Legitimate"],"a":2},{"k":"mcq","q":"Power from a position in a formal hierarchy is…","o":["Legitimate","Reward","Coercive","Referent"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which conflict strategy results in a win-win situation?","o":["Compromising","Collaborating","Accommodating","Forcing"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"Compromising typically results in a lose-lose situation on the slides.","a":true},{"k":"mcq","q":"Pushing your viewpoint through a power position to resolve an emergency is…","o":["Avoiding","Forcing","Collaborating","Accommodating"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"Conflict is always harmful to a project.","a":false,"w":"It is not inherently good or bad; well handled it leads to creative solutions and stronger relationships."},{"k":"mcq","q":"Core output of Manage Communications?","o":["Communications management plan","Project communications","Work performance information","Change log"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Core outputs of Monitor Communications?","o":["Project communications","Work performance information and change requests","Communications plan","Stakeholder register"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"The communications management plan includes ___ processes for issues that cannot be resolved at a lower level.","a":"escalation"},{"k":"tf","q":"A virtual team must be spread across different countries.","a":false,"w":"Virtual means little or no face-to-face contact; the team can be local."},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a challenge of global and virtual teams?","o":["Wider talent pool","Compliance with local labour laws","Saving office costs","Including people with disabilities"],"a":1}]},{"id":"t6","n":"Topic 6","title":"Project Initiation and Planning","why":"How organisations choose projects, the business case, the charter, and the project management plan.","cards":[{"h":"Why organisations start projects","b":"\n  <p>Four factors: (1) <b>regulatory, legal or social</b> requirements; (2) <b>stakeholder requests</b>; (3) <b>strategic</b> opportunities or business needs; (4) to <b>create, improve or fix</b> products, processes or services.</p>"},{"h":"Pre-project documents","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Needs assessment</span><em>→</em><span>Business case</span><em>→</em><span>Benefits management plan</span><em>→</em><span>Project charter</span><em>→</em><span>PM plan</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th>Document</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Needs assessment</td><td>Identify problems/opportunities (SWOT, PESTEL)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Business case</td><td>Justify the project: options incl. <b>\"do nothing\"</b>, ROI, <b>payback period</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Benefits management plan</td><td>How and when benefits are delivered: <b>benefits owner</b>, metrics, timeframe</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"The project charter","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project charter</b>The document issued by the <b>sponsor</b> that <b>formally authorises</b> the project and gives the PM authority to apply organisational resources.</div>\n  <p>Contains (high level): purpose, objectives and success criteria, high-level requirements and scope, risks, milestone schedule, budget, stakeholders, exit criteria, PM name and authority, sponsor.</p>\n  <p><b>Initiating group:</b> Develop Project Charter (→ charter) + Identify Stakeholders (→ stakeholder register).</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">CA1: the sponsor does NOT develop the team charter (the team does).</div>"},{"h":"The project management plan","b":"\n  <p>Describes how the project will be <b>executed, monitored, controlled and closed</b>. It integrates all subsidiary plans (scope, schedule, cost, quality, resource, communications, risk, procurement, stakeholder) and the <b>baselines</b> (scope, schedule, cost → together the performance measurement baseline).</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Charter</th><th>PM plan</th></tr><tr><td>High level; authorises</td><td>Detailed; how the work will be done</td></tr><tr><td>Initiating</td><td>Planning</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"The four factors: slide examples","b":"<table><tr><th>Example</th><th>Factor</th></tr><tr><td>Bank adds new customer authentication to meet <b>Central Bank</b> standards</td><td>Regulatory, legal or social requirement</td></tr><tr><td>New payroll system requested by the <b>CFO</b></td><td>Stakeholder request</td></tr><tr><td>Hospital health system for remote care</td><td>Business or technological strategy</td></tr><tr><td>Airport queue system cutting waits by 50%</td><td>Create, improve or fix products or processes</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Initiation in three stages","b":"<div class=\"flow\"><span>Pre-project work: needs assessment, business case, benefits plan</span><em>→</em><span>Starting the project: charter</span><em>→</em><span>Organising and preparing: PM plan</span></div><p><b>Needs assessment</b> contains: business goals · problems or opportunities · affected stakeholders · scope · recommended actions. It is an <b>input to the business case</b>. Tools: SWOT, PESTEL.</p>"},{"h":"Business case and benefits plan: contents","b":"<p><b>Business case</b> (developed before the project, <b>reviewed periodically</b>): business need · problem/opportunity statement · options incl. \"do nothing\" · recommended option · strategic alignment · cost-benefit (ROI, payback) · known risks · <b>critical success factors</b> · success measures · implementation approach (milestones, dependencies).</p><p><b>Benefits management plan</b>: target benefits (tangible and intangible) · strategic alignment · timeframe · benefits owner · metrics · <b>assumptions</b> · risks.</p>"},{"h":"Charter elements: the full slide list","b":"<ul><li>Purpose · measurable objectives and success criteria</li><li>High-level requirements, description, boundaries, key deliverables</li><li>Overall project risk · summary milestone schedule</li><li><b>Pre-approved financial resources</b> · key stakeholder list</li><li><b>Approval requirements</b> (what is success, who signs off)</li><li><b>Exit criteria</b> (conditions to close or cancel)</li><li>Assigned PM, responsibility and authority level</li><li>Name and authority of the sponsor</li></ul><p>Charter inputs: business case, agreements, EEFs, OPAs. Outputs: charter, assumption log.</p>"},{"h":"PM plan components: the full list","b":"<table><tr><th>Subsidiary plans</th><th>Baselines and others</th></tr><tr><td>Scope, <b>requirements</b>, schedule, cost, quality, resource, communications, risk, procurement, stakeholder</td><td>Scope, schedule, cost baselines; performance measurement baseline</td></tr><tr><td><b>Change management</b> plan, <b>configuration management</b> plan</td><td>Project life cycle description; <b>development approach</b></td></tr></table><div class=\"trap\">Conclusion slide: the business case, benefits plan, charter and PM plan must stay <b>aligned</b> for the whole life cycle.</div>"}],"recall":[["Name the four factors that lead organisations to start projects.","Regulatory/legal/social requirements; stakeholder requests; strategy; create, improve or fix products/processes."],["Which document includes the \"do nothing\" option and payback period?","The business case."],["Who owns the benefits in the benefits management plan?","The benefits owner."],["Who issues the project charter?","The sponsor."],["What does the charter do?","Formally authorises the project and gives the PM authority to use resources."],["Charter vs PM plan?","Charter: high-level authorisation (Initiating). PM plan: detailed how-to with baselines (Planning)."],["What three baselines make up the performance measurement baseline?","Scope, schedule and cost baselines."],["A bank changes its authentication to meet Central Bank standards. Which factor?","Meet regulatory, legal or social requirements."],["Name the three stages of project initiation.","Pre-project work; starting the project (charter); organising and preparing (PM plan)."],["What does a needs assessment contain?","Business goals, problems or opportunities, affected stakeholders, scope, recommended actions."],["What two frameworks help a needs assessment?","SWOT and PESTEL."],["Is the business case reviewed after the project starts?","Yes, periodically."],["Name four business case contents.","Business need, problem statement, options incl. do nothing, recommended option, alignment, cost-benefit, risks, critical success factors, success measures, implementation approach (any four)."],["What are project exit criteria?","Conditions to be met to close or cancel the project or phase."],["Name two PM plan components that are not knowledge-area plans or baselines.","Change management plan, configuration management plan, life cycle description, development approach (any two)."],["Key output of Identify Stakeholders?","The stakeholder register."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the documents produced before and during project initiation, from needs assessment to the project charter.","pts":["Needs assessment (SWOT/PESTEL)","Business case (do nothing, ROI, payback)","Benefits management plan (owner, metrics, timeframe)","Charter: sponsor, authorises, PM authority","Correct order","Example"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Needs assessment</b> — identifies the problem or opportunity using tools such as SWOT and PESTEL.</li><li><b>Business case</b> — justifies the investment: compares options including \"do nothing\", and estimates ROI and payback period.</li><li><b>Benefits management plan</b> — states how and when benefits will be delivered and measured, the benefits owner, metrics and timeframe.</li><li><b>Project charter</b> — issued by the sponsor; formally authorises the project, names the PM and their authority, and records high-level objectives, scope, risks, milestones, budget and exit criteria.</li></ol><p>The PM plan then details how the project will be executed, monitored and controlled.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"An airport installs a queue system that cuts waits by 50%. Factor?","o":["Regulatory requirement","Stakeholder request","Create, improve or fix products, processes or services","Technological strategy"],"a":2},{"k":"mcq","q":"A new payroll system requested by the CFO is…","o":["A stakeholder request","A regulatory requirement","A strategic change","A fix"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which document includes a \"do nothing\" option?","o":["Charter","Business case","Benefits management plan","PM plan"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"The needs assessment is an input to the…","o":["Business case","PM plan","Stakeholder register","WBS"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"Who issues the project charter?","o":["The project manager","The sponsor","The team","The PMO"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"The sponsor develops the team charter.","a":false,"w":"The team develops the team charter; the sponsor issues the project charter."},{"k":"gap","q":"PESTEL stands for Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Environmental and ___.","a":"legal"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Who is accountable for monitoring and reporting realised benefits?","o":["Sponsor","Benefits owner","PMO","Customer"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Conditions to close or cancel the project are the…","o":["Success criteria","Exit criteria","Approval requirements","Milestones"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is NOT in the initiating process group?","o":["Develop Project Charter","Identify Stakeholders","Develop Project Management Plan","Both A and B are initiating"],"a":2},{"k":"tf","q":"The business case, benefits plan, charter and PM plan must remain aligned throughout the project.","a":true},{"k":"gap","q":"Scope, schedule and cost baselines together form the performance ___ baseline.","a":"measurement"}]},{"id":"t7","n":"Topic 7","title":"Managing Project Scope","why":"Scope is the fence around the project. Everything else (schedule, cost, risk, quality) is built on it.","cards":[{"h":"The big idea","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Definition — Project Scope Management</b>The processes required to ensure that the project includes <u>all the work required, and only the work required</u>, to complete the project successfully. It is primarily concerned with <b>defining and controlling</b> what is and is not included in the project.</div>\n  <p><b>\"All\"</b> protects you from <i>missing</i> work. <b>\"Only\"</b> protects you from <i>extra</i> work (scope creep). Students forget \"only\".</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Project scope</th><th>Product scope</th></tr><tr><td>The <b>work</b> done to deliver the product</td><td>The <b>features and functions</b> of the product itself</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Scope = the fence. Inside the fence: do it. Outside: don't, unless it's formally changed.</div>"},{"h":"The 6 scope processes (in order)","b":"\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Plan Scope Mgmt</span><em>→</em><span>Collect Requirements</span><em>→</em><span>Define Scope</span><em>→</em><span>Create WBS</span><em>→</em><span>Validate Scope</span><em>→</em><span>Control Scope</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Key output (learn these!)</th><th>Group</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Plan Scope Management</td><td>Scope management plan + Requirements management plan</td><td>Planning</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Collect Requirements</td><td>Requirements documentation + Requirements traceability matrix (RTM)</td><td>Planning</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Define Scope</td><td>Project scope statement</td><td>Planning</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Create WBS</td><td>Scope baseline</td><td>Planning</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Validate Scope</td><td>Accepted deliverables</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Control Scope</td><td>Work performance information + change requests</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>P</b>lease <b>C</b>ollect <b>D</b>ata, <b>C</b>reate <b>V</b>alid <b>C</b>ontrols\" → Plan, Collect, Define, Create WBS, Validate, Control.</div>"},{"h":"Plan Scope Management + Collect Requirements","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Plan Scope Management</b>The process of creating a scope management plan that documents how the project and product scope will be defined, validated and controlled.</div>\n  <p>Outputs: <b>scope management plan</b> (how scope will be defined, developed, monitored, controlled, validated) and <b>requirements management plan</b> (how requirements will be analysed, documented, managed). Both are \"HOW\" plans — neither lists actual requirements.</p>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Collect Requirements</b>The process of determining, documenting and managing stakeholder needs and requirements to meet objectives. It provides the basis for defining the product scope and project scope.</div>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> Data gathering — brainstorming, interviews, focus groups, questionnaires & surveys (benchmarking on the tools slide) · Decision making — voting, multicriteria decision analysis · Data representation — affinity diagrams, mind mapping · Context diagram · <b>Prototypes</b> (working model for early feedback).</p>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b> Requirements documentation (what is required) and the <b>Requirements Traceability Matrix</b> — a grid linking each requirement <b>from its origin to the deliverable that satisfies it</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"grid linking requirements to deliverables\" → RTM</div>"},{"h":"Define Scope → Project Scope Statement","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Define Scope</b>The process of developing a detailed description of the project and product, describing the product, service or result boundaries and acceptance criteria.</div>\n  <p>Output: <b>Project scope statement</b> — the description of the project scope, major deliverables, assumptions and constraints. Four key components:</p>\n  <ul><li><b>Project scope description</b> (progressively elaborated — starts high level, gets detailed)</li><li><b>Project deliverables</b></li><li><b>Acceptance criteria</b> (conditions to be met before a deliverable is accepted — used later in Validate Scope)</li><li><b>Project exclusions</b> (what is OUT of scope)</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Why exclusions matter:</b> stating what is out of scope manages stakeholder expectations and reduces scope creep. E.g. \"The mobile app is not included.\"</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Requirements documentation (needs) ≠ Scope statement (agreed boundary) ≠ WBS (work broken down).</div>"},{"h":"Create WBS → Scope Baseline","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Create WBS</b>The process of subdividing project deliverables and project work into smaller, more manageable components. It provides a framework of what has to be delivered.</div>\n  <p>Tool: <b>Decomposition</b>. Levels: Project → major deliverables/phases → sub-deliverables → <b>work packages</b> (lowest level).</p>\n  <ul><li>Each work package has a <b>unique ID</b> (e.g. 1.2.1) → forms a <b>code of accounts</b> for summing costs, schedule and resources.</li>\n  <li>Includes <b>all</b> work, including project management work (100% rule).</li>\n  <li><b>Scope baseline</b> = approved scope statement + WBS + WBS dictionary. Changed <b>only through formal change control</b>; used as the basis for comparison.</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's example (software project):</b> 1 Planning · 2 Analysis · 3 Design (3.1 Architecture, 3.2 Database, 3.3 Interface) · 4 Development (4.1 Frontend, 4.2 Backend, 4.3 Database) · 5 Testing (unit, integration, UAT) · 6 Deployment (deploy, training, monitoring).</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">WBS = <b>WHAT</b> work. Schedule = <b>WHEN</b>. A WBS has no dates.</div>"},{"h":"Validate Scope vs Control Scope","b":"\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Validate Scope</th><th>Control Scope</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Definition</td><td>Formalizing <b>acceptance</b> of the completed project deliverables</td><td><b>Monitoring</b> the status of project and product scope and <b>managing changes</b> to the scope baseline</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Who</td><td>Customer / sponsor signs off</td><td>Project team, throughout</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Key tool</td><td><b>Inspection</b> (examining deliverables against acceptance criteria)</td><td><b>Variance analysis</b> (baseline vs actual) and trend analysis</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Key input</td><td>Verified deliverables (from Control Quality)</td><td>Work performance data</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Key output</td><td><b>Accepted deliverables</b>, WPI, change requests</td><td>Work performance information, change requests</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Work performance data</b> = raw (\"12 of 30 done\"). <b>Work performance information</b> = analysed against baseline (\"40% done vs 55% planned — behind\").</p>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"formal acceptance by the customer\" → Validate Scope · \"compare baseline to actual\" → Control Scope</div>"},{"h":"Scope creep","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Scope creep</b>The uncontrolled expansion to project or product scope without adjustments to time, cost and resources.</div>\n  <p><b>Consequences:</b> budget overruns, missed deadlines, reduced quality.</p>\n  <p><b>Control measures (lecturer):</b></p>\n  <ol><li><b>Clear scope definition</b> — scope statement with explicit exclusions; the yardstick for \"in or out\".</li><li><b>Change control process</b> — every change request goes through Perform Integrated Change Control for review and approval.</li></ol>\n  <p><b>Example:</b> A client casually asks the developer to \"add one more report\"; the developer agrees without telling the PM → creep. If it's logged, assessed and approved with new time/cost → <b>approved change, NOT creep</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Creep = growing without permission.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Scope creep also appears as a <b>scheduling problem</b> (Topic 8) and a <b>scope risk</b> (Topic 10). Use it to link topics in essay answers.</div>"},{"h":"Scope tools from the ITTO slides","b":"<table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Extra T&T on the slides</th></tr><tr><td>Plan Scope Mgmt</td><td>Expert judgement, <b>alternative analysis</b>, meetings (inputs: charter, quality plan, life cycle description, development approach)</td></tr><tr><td>Collect Requirements</td><td><b>Document analysis</b>, <b>benchmarking</b>, interpersonal skills (inputs include the <b>business case</b> and stakeholder register)</td></tr><tr><td>Define Scope</td><td>Alternative analysis, multicriteria decision analysis, facilitation, <b>product analysis</b></td></tr><tr><td>Validate Scope</td><td>Inspection, <b>voting</b></td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Requirements documentation and RTM: detail","b":"<p><b>Requirements documentation</b> describes how each requirement meets the business need. Format ranges from a simple list by stakeholder and <b>priority</b> to an elaborate document with an executive summary and attachments.</p><p>The <b>RTM</b> tracks requirements through the whole life cycle, so that requirements approved in the documentation are actually <b>delivered at the end</b>.</p><p>The scope statement documents the <b>entire scope</b>: project and product scope.</p>"},{"h":"Validate Scope outputs explained","b":"<p>Validate Scope brings <b>objectivity</b> to acceptance and raises the chance the final result is accepted.</p><ul><li><b>Accepted deliverables</b>: validated by the <b>customer or sponsor</b> as meeting acceptance criteria.</li><li><b>WPI</b>: which deliverables were accepted, which were not, and <b>why</b>.</li><li><b>Change requests</b>: unaccepted deliverables may need <b>defect repair</b>, processed through Perform Integrated Change Control.</li></ul><p><b>Inspection</b> = measuring, examining and validating work against requirements and acceptance criteria. <b>Variance analysis</b> checks if the variance is within <b>threshold</b> or needs corrective or preventive action.</p>"}],"recall":[["Define project scope management.","The processes required to ensure the project includes all the work required, and only the work required, to complete the project successfully; concerned with defining and controlling what is and is not included."],["Name the 6 scope management processes in order.","Plan Scope Management, Collect Requirements, Define Scope, Create WBS, Validate Scope, Control Scope."],["What is a Requirements Traceability Matrix?","A grid that links product requirements from their origin to the deliverables that satisfy them (output of Collect Requirements)."],["What are the 4 key components of the project scope statement?","Project scope description (progressively elaborated), project deliverables, acceptance criteria, project exclusions."],["What makes up the scope baseline?","The approved project scope statement + WBS + WBS dictionary. Changed only through formal change control."],["What is the lowest level of a WBS called?","Work package."],["Main tool of Create WBS?","Decomposition."],["Main tool of Validate Scope? Its key output?","Inspection. Accepted deliverables."],["Main tool of Control Scope?","Variance analysis (comparing baseline to actual), plus trend analysis."],["Define scope creep + 2 control measures.","Uncontrolled expansion of project/product scope without adjustments to time, cost and resources. Controls: clear scope definition (incl. exclusions) and a change control process."],["What extra data gathering technique appears on the scope tools slide?","Benchmarking (with brainstorming, interviews, focus groups, questionnaires and surveys)."],["Which tool is unique to Define Scope on the slides?","Product analysis."],["What does the RTM ensure?","That requirements approved in the requirements documentation are delivered at the end of the project."],["Who validates accepted deliverables?","The customer or sponsor."],["Why might Validate Scope produce change requests?","Completed deliverables not formally accepted may need defect repair."],["What does variance analysis decide in Control Scope?","Whether the variance is within threshold or corrective/preventive action is needed."]],"theory":[{"q":"Define project scope management and briefly explain its six processes.","pts":["Definition with \"all the work required, and only the work required\"","Mentions defining and controlling what is/is not included","Plan Scope Management → scope mgmt plan + requirements mgmt plan","Collect Requirements → requirements documentation + RTM","Define Scope → project scope statement","Create WBS → decomposition, work packages, scope baseline","Validate Scope → formal acceptance, accepted deliverables","Control Scope → monitor status, manage changes to scope baseline"],"model":"<p><b>Project scope management</b> is the set of processes required to ensure that the project includes all the work required, and only the work required, to complete it successfully. It is concerned with defining and controlling what is and is not included in the project.</p><ol><li><b>Plan Scope Management</b> — creates the scope management plan (how scope will be defined, validated and controlled) and the requirements management plan.</li><li><b>Collect Requirements</b> — determines, documents and manages stakeholder needs using interviews, focus groups, surveys, prototypes etc.; outputs requirements documentation and the requirements traceability matrix.</li><li><b>Define Scope</b> — develops a detailed description of the project and product; output is the project scope statement (description, deliverables, acceptance criteria, exclusions).</li><li><b>Create WBS</b> — subdivides deliverables and work into smaller, manageable components (work packages) by decomposition; output is the scope baseline.</li><li><b>Validate Scope</b> — formalizes acceptance of completed deliverables by the customer/sponsor through inspection; output is accepted deliverables.</li><li><b>Control Scope</b> — monitors the status of scope and manages changes to the scope baseline using variance analysis.</li></ol><p>Together these prevent both missing work and scope creep.</p>"},{"q":"What is scope creep? Explain its consequences and two ways a project manager can control it, with an example.","pts":["Definition: uncontrolled expansion…without adjusting time, cost, resources","Consequences: budget overrun, missed deadlines, reduced quality","Control 1: clear scope definition / scope statement with exclusions","Control 2: change control process (Perform Integrated Change Control)","Relevant example","Distinguishes creep from an approved change"],"model":"<p><b>Scope creep</b> is the uncontrolled expansion of project or product scope without adjustments to time, cost and resources.</p><p><b>Consequences:</b> budget overruns, missed deadlines and reduced quality, because extra work is absorbed without extra time or money.</p><p><b>Controls:</b> (1) <b>Clear scope definition</b> — a detailed scope statement with explicit exclusions so every request can be judged as in or out of scope. (2) <b>Change control process</b> — all change requests go through Perform Integrated Change Control to be reviewed, approved or rejected, with time and cost adjusted.</p><p><b>Example:</b> during a school portal project a lecturer asks a developer to add an attendance report; the developer adds it informally, delaying testing. That is creep. If it had been logged as a change request and approved with a revised schedule, it would be a managed change, not creep.</p>"},{"q":"Differentiate between Validate Scope and Control Scope.","pts":["Validate = formal acceptance of completed deliverables","Validate done by customer/sponsor; tool inspection","Validate output: accepted deliverables","Control = monitor scope status + manage changes to scope baseline","Control tool: variance (and trend) analysis","Control outputs: work performance information, change requests"],"model":"<table><tr><th>Validate Scope</th><th>Control Scope</th></tr><tr><td>Process of formalizing acceptance of completed project deliverables.</td><td>Process of monitoring the status of project and product scope and managing changes to the scope baseline.</td></tr><tr><td>Focus: acceptance by customer/sponsor.</td><td>Focus: keeping the scope baseline intact; preventing creep.</td></tr><tr><td>Tool: inspection (and voting).</td><td>Tools: variance analysis, trend analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Input: verified deliverables from Control Quality.</td><td>Input: work performance data.</td></tr><tr><td>Outputs: accepted deliverables, WPI, change requests.</td><td>Outputs: WPI, change requests, plan updates.</td></tr></table>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"Which process produces the requirements traceability matrix?","o":["Define Scope","Collect Requirements","Create WBS","Validate Scope"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"The scope baseline consists of the scope statement, WBS and…","o":["Project charter","WBS dictionary","RTM","Schedule"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"The lowest level of the WBS is the work ___.","a":"package"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Key tool of Validate Scope?","o":["Variance analysis","Inspection","Decomposition","Benchmarking"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Validate Scope belongs to which process group?","o":["Planning","Executing","Monitoring and Controlling","Closing"],"a":2},{"k":"tf","q":"A client's extra request that is logged, assessed and approved with new time and cost is scope creep.","a":false,"w":"That is an approved change; creep is uncontrolled."},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which component of the scope statement states what is OUT of scope?","o":["Acceptance criteria","Project exclusions","Deliverables","Scope description"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Scope creep is the ___ expansion of scope without adjustments to time, cost and resources.","a":"uncontrolled"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Main output of Define Scope?","o":["Scope baseline","Project scope statement","Requirements documentation","Accepted deliverables"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"The WBS includes project management work.","a":true},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a data representation technique in Collect Requirements?","o":["Affinity diagrams","Inspection","Variance analysis","Decomposition"],"a":0}]},{"id":"t8","n":"Topic 8","title":"Managing Project Scheduling","why":"Scheduling turns the WBS into dates. CPM, PERT and crashing vs fast tracking are the most testable parts.","cards":[{"h":"Definition + 6 processes","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project Schedule Management</b>The processes required to manage the timely completion of the project. Scheduling provides a detailed plan that represents <b>how and when</b> the project will deliver the products, services and results defined in the project scope.</div>\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Plan Schedule Mgmt</span><em>→</em><span>Define Activities</span><em>→</em><span>Sequence Activities</span><em>→</em><span>Estimate Durations</span><em>→</em><span>Develop Schedule</span><em>→</em><span>Control Schedule</span></div>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>P</b>lease <b>D</b>on't <b>S</b>top <b>E</b>ating <b>D</b>elicious <b>C</b>ake.\"</div>\n  <p><b>Plan Schedule Management</b> → <b>schedule management plan</b> (policies, procedures, criteria for developing, monitoring and controlling the schedule). It is the rules, NOT the timetable.</p>"},{"h":"Define Activities","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Define Activities</b>The process of identifying and documenting the specific actions to be performed to produce the project deliverables. It <b>decomposes work packages into activities</b>.</div>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> expert judgement, decomposition, <b>rolling wave planning</b> (near-term work planned in detail, later work at a higher level), meetings.</p>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b></p><ul><li><b>Activity list</b> — every activity with an ID and scope of work description</li><li><b>Activity attributes</b> — extend each activity (ID, WBS ID, name, predecessors/successors…)</li><li><b>Milestone list</b> — significant points or events. <b>Milestones have zero duration.</b></li></ul>\n  <p>Example: work package \"Database design\" → activities: draft ER diagram, normalise tables, review with DBA.</p>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"zero duration\" → milestone</div>"},{"h":"Sequence Activities — PDM","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Sequence Activities</b>The process of identifying and documenting relationships among project activities; it defines the logical sequence of work for the greatest efficiency. Output: <b>project schedule network diagram</b>.</div>\n  <p>Tool: <b>Precedence Diagramming Method (PDM)</b>. A = predecessor, B = successor:</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>FS</b> Finish-to-Start (most common)</td><td>B can't start until A finishes</td><td>Code login → then test login</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>SS</b> Start-to-Start</td><td>B can't start until A starts</td><td>Write test cases once coding starts</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>FF</b> Finish-to-Finish</td><td>B can't finish until A finishes</td><td>Documentation finishes when coding finishes</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>SF</b> Start-to-Finish (rare)</td><td>B can't finish until A starts</td><td>Old system can't shut down until new one starts</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Lead</b> = successor starts early (acceleration). <b>Lag</b> = waiting time (delay), e.g. wait 3 days for concrete to cure.</p>"},{"h":"Estimate Activity Durations","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Estimate Activity Durations</b>The process of estimating the number of work periods needed to complete individual activities with estimated resources.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Idea</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Analogous</td><td>Use a <b>similar past</b> project/activity. Quick, less accurate.</td><td>Last portal took 6 months → ~6 months</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Parametric</td><td><b>Algorithm/statistical relationship</b> with a variable</td><td>100 lines/day × 5,000 lines → 50 days</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Three-point</td><td>Optimistic, most likely, pessimistic (basis of PERT)</td><td>O=2, M=3, P=4</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Bottom-up</td><td>Estimate lowest level, <b>add upward</b>. Most accurate, slowest.</td><td>Sum of activity estimates</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b> duration estimates + <b>basis of estimates</b> (how the estimate was derived). Also reserve analysis is a tool.</p>"},{"h":"Develop Schedule + Gantt","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Develop Schedule</b>The process of analyzing activity sequences, durations, resource requirements and schedule constraints to create a schedule model for project execution and monitoring and controlling.</div>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b></p><ul><li><b>Schedule baseline</b> — the <b>approved</b> version of the schedule model; changed only through formal change control; used to compare against actual results.</li><li><b>Project schedule</b> — linked activities with planned dates, durations, milestones and resources.</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> schedule network analysis, <b>critical path method</b>, resource optimization, what-if scenario analysis, simulation, leads & lags, <b>schedule compression</b>, PMIS, agile release planning.</p>\n  <p><b>Gantt chart:</b> bar chart on a calendar showing task dependencies, durations and critical path. (Network diagram = logic; Gantt = timeline.)</p>"},{"h":"Critical Path Method (CPM) — worked","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>CPM</b>A technique used to estimate the minimum project duration and determine the amount of schedule flexibility (float) on the logical network paths.</div>\n  <p><b>Critical path = the LONGEST path</b> through the network = the <b>shortest possible project duration</b>. Usually zero float. Delay a critical activity → the whole project is delayed.</p>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's school example</b> (buy & install computers + solar):</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Days</th><th>After</th></tr><tr><td>A Budget</td><td>2</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>B Purchase</td><td>4</td><td>A</td></tr><tr><td>C Install computers</td><td>1</td><td>B</td></tr><tr><td>D Install solar</td><td>3</td><td>B</td></tr><tr><td>E Test computers</td><td>1</td><td>C</td></tr><tr><td>F Test solar</td><td>2</td><td>D</td></tr><tr><td>G Deploy</td><td>1</td><td>E, F</td></tr></table>\n  <p>Path A–B–C–E–G = 2+4+1+1+1 = <b>9</b> · Path A–B–D–F–G = 2+4+3+2+1 = <b>12 ← critical</b>. Float on C and E = 12 − 9 = <b>3 days</b>.</p>\n  <p><b>Steps:</b> forward pass → ES, EF · backward pass → LS, LF · <b>Total Float = LS − ES = LF − EF</b> · activities with TF = 0 form the critical path.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Critical path is the LONGEST path, not the shortest. Crashing a non-critical task does NOT shorten the project.</div>"},{"h":"PERT — worked","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>PERT</b>A framework using three time estimates per task — optimistic (O), most likely (M), pessimistic (P) — to handle uncertain timelines and find the critical path.</div>\n  <p style=\"font-size:22px;text-align:center\"><b>E = (O + 4M + P) ÷ 6</b></p>\n  <p>Lecturer's example, same school project: A (2,3,4) → 3.0 · B (3,4,6) → 4.2 · D (2,3,5) → 3.2 · F (2,3,4) → 3.0 · G (1,2,3) → 2.0. Critical path A–B–D–F–G = <b>15.4 days</b> (other path 13.2).</p>\n  <table><tr><th>CPM</th><th>PERT</th></tr><tr><td>One fixed duration per activity</td><td>Three estimates, weighted average</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty not modelled</td><td>Uncertainty modelled</td></tr><tr><td>Known, repeated work</td><td>New / uncertain work</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">The Most likely counts 4 times: \"four in the middle\".</div>"},{"h":"Control Schedule + common problems","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Control Schedule</b>The process of monitoring the status of the project to update the project schedule and managing changes to the schedule baseline.</div>\n  <p>Outputs: <b>work performance information</b>, <b>schedule forecasts</b>, <b>change requests</b>. Tools: variance analysis, earned value analysis, iteration burndown chart, trend analysis, CPM, compression, resource optimization.</p>\n  <h3>Common scheduling problems → controls</h3>\n  <table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Control</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Scope creep (unplanned scope changes)</td><td>Clear scope statement + change control</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Resource management issues</td><td>Resource optimization / allocation</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Tracking tasks & deadlines</td><td>Project management software</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Communication inadequacies</td><td>A broad communication plan</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Unrealistic deadlines (wrong assumptions)</td><td>Better estimation; schedule compression</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Schedule compression: crashing vs fast tracking","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Schedule compression</b>Techniques used to shorten the schedule duration <b>without reducing the project scope</b>, to meet schedule constraints or imposed dates.</div>\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Crashing</th><th>Fast tracking</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>How</td><td><b>Add resources</b> (overtime, extra staff, pay to expedite) for the least incremental cost</td><td>Do activities normally done in sequence <b>in parallel</b> (overlap)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Side effect</td><td><b>Cost ↑</b></td><td><b>Risk ↑</b> and possible rework</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Example</td><td>Hire 2 extra developers for coding</td><td>Start front-end before database design is fully finished</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">CRASH = throw money/people at it. FAST-TRACK = overlap.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Cutting features is scope reduction, not compression. Compression only works on the critical path.</div>"},{"h":"Schedule definitions you may be asked","b":"<ul><li><b>Plan Schedule Management</b>: establishes policies, procedures and documentation for planning, developing, managing and controlling the schedule.</li><li><b>Define Activities</b> output is the basis for estimating, scheduling, executing, monitoring and controlling.</li><li><b>Network diagram</b>: graphic of logical relationships (<b>dependencies</b>); manual or by software.</li><li><b>Duration estimates</b>: quantitative assessments of the likely number of time periods.</li><li><b>Schedule forecasts</b>: predictions of future conditions and events based on current knowledge.</li></ul>"},{"h":"Schedule ITTO extras","b":"<table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Extras on the slides</th></tr><tr><td>Sequence Activities</td><td>PDM, <b>dependency determination and integration</b>, leads and lags, PMIS</td></tr><tr><td>Estimate Durations</td><td>Analogous, parametric, three-point, bottom-up; <b>alternative and reserve analysis</b>; decision making</td></tr><tr><td>Develop Schedule</td><td>Outputs also include <b>schedule data</b> and <b>project calendars</b></td></tr><tr><td>Control Schedule</td><td><b>Performance reviews</b>, what-if analysis, burndown chart, earned value</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"CPM Example 1 from the slides","b":"<p>Paths from start to finish:</p><table><tr><th>Path</th><th>Days</th></tr><tr><td>A (5) → D (10)</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>B (8) → E (7) → D (10)</td><td>25</td></tr><tr><td>C (15) → F (20)</td><td><b>35: critical</b></td></tr></table><p>Project duration = 35 days. Path B-E-D has 10 days of float; A-D has 20.</p><div class=\"trap\">The float figures are worked from the path lengths; the slide states only the critical path.</div>"},{"h":"PERT: steps and the full table","b":"<ol><li>Define tasks</li><li>Identify dependencies</li><li>Compute E = (O + 4M + P) ÷ 6</li><li>Draw the network diagram</li><li>Identify the critical path</li></ol><p>School example, missing rows: C install computers (1, 2, 3) → <b>2.0</b>; E test computers (1, 2, 3) → <b>2.0</b>. Path A-B-C-E-G = 3.0 + 4.2 + 2.0 + 2.0 + 2.0 = <b>13.2</b>.</p><p>Plot the critical path on a <b>Gantt chart</b>; scheduling software can draw both.</p>"},{"h":"Crashing examples and fast-tracking rule","b":"<p><b>Crashing</b> examples on the slides: approving <b>overtime</b> · adding people or equipment · <b>paying to expedite</b> delivery to critical-path activities.</p><p><b>Fast tracking</b> only works when activities on the <b>critical path</b> can be overlapped.</p><p>Conclusion slide: proper estimation of resources and durations is critical; software helps planning and tracking.</p>"}],"recall":[["Define project schedule management.","The processes required to manage the timely completion of the project; scheduling gives a detailed plan of how and when the project will deliver what is defined in the scope."],["Name the 6 schedule processes.","Plan Schedule Management, Define Activities, Sequence Activities, Estimate Activity Durations, Develop Schedule, Control Schedule."],["What are the 3 main outputs of Define Activities?","Activity list, activity attributes, milestone list."],["What is the duration of a milestone?","Zero."],["Name the 4 PDM relationships. Which is most common?","Finish-to-Start (most common), Start-to-Start, Finish-to-Finish, Start-to-Finish (rarest)."],["What is the critical path?","The longest path through the network; it gives the shortest possible project duration; activities on it usually have zero float."],["Formula for total float?","TF = LS − ES = LF − EF."],["PERT formula?","E = (O + 4M + P) / 6."],["Crashing vs fast tracking?","Crashing adds resources (cost rises). Fast tracking overlaps sequential activities (risk rises). Neither reduces scope."],["What is the schedule baseline?","The approved version of the schedule model, changed only through formal change control, used as the basis for comparison with actual results."],["Analogous vs parametric estimating?","Analogous uses a similar past project. Parametric uses a statistical relationship/algorithm with parameters (e.g. lines of code per day)."],["Define Plan Schedule Management.","Establishing the policies, procedures and documentation for planning, developing, managing and controlling the project schedule."],["What is a project schedule network diagram?","A graphical representation of the logical relationships (dependencies) among schedule activities."],["Name the five PERT steps.","Define tasks, identify dependencies, compute expected times, draw the network diagram, identify the critical path."],["What are schedule forecasts?","Predictions of future project conditions and events based on information available at the time."],["Give three crashing examples.","Approving overtime, adding people or equipment, paying to expedite delivery."],["Name two outputs of Develop Schedule besides the baseline and schedule.","Schedule data, project calendars (also change requests)."]],"theory":[{"q":"Explain the Critical Path Method. Using an example, show how the critical path and float are determined.","pts":["Definition: minimum project duration + float","Critical path = longest path = shortest project duration","Zero float on critical path; delay delays project","Forward pass (ES/EF) and backward pass (LS/LF)","TF = LS − ES or LF − EF","Worked example with paths summed correctly","Float of non-critical path calculated"],"model":"<p>The <b>Critical Path Method</b> is a technique used to estimate the minimum project duration and determine the amount of schedule flexibility (float) on the network paths. The <b>critical path</b> is the longest path through the network, so it gives the shortest possible time to finish the project. Activities on it normally have zero total float: any delay to them delays the project.</p><p><b>Steps:</b> list activities, durations and predecessors; draw the network; forward pass to find early start/finish; backward pass to find late start/finish; total float = LS − ES (or LF − EF); activities with zero float form the critical path.</p><p><b>Example:</b> A(2) → B(4) → C(1) → E(1) → G(1) and B → D(3) → F(2) → G. Path A–B–C–E–G = 9 days; A–B–D–F–G = 12 days. The critical path is A–B–D–F–G and the project takes 12 days. C and E have 12 − 9 = 3 days of float.</p>"},{"q":"Differentiate between crashing and fast tracking as schedule compression techniques.","pts":["Compression = shorten duration without reducing scope","Crashing = add resources, least incremental cost","Crashing increases cost","Fast tracking = sequential activities done in parallel","Fast tracking increases risk/rework","Example of each","Both applied to the critical path"],"model":"<p><b>Schedule compression</b> shortens schedule duration without reducing project scope.</p><table><tr><th>Crashing</th><th>Fast tracking</th></tr><tr><td>Shortens duration for the least incremental cost by adding resources: overtime, extra people, paying to expedite delivery.</td><td>Activities or phases normally done in sequence are performed in parallel for at least part of their duration.</td></tr><tr><td>Increases cost.</td><td>Increases risk and possible rework.</td></tr><tr><td>E.g. hiring two extra developers.</td><td>E.g. starting front-end coding before database design is complete.</td></tr></table><p>Both must be applied to critical-path activities; compressing a non-critical activity does not shorten the project.</p>"},{"q":"Discuss five common problems in project scheduling and how each can be controlled.","pts":["Scope creep → clear scope + change control","Resource management issues → resource allocation/optimization","Tracking tasks and deadlines → PM software","Communication inadequacies → communication plan","Unrealistic deadlines → better estimates / compression","Explains each, not just lists"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Scope creep</b> — unplanned changes in scope add work without extra time. Control: a clear scope statement and a change control process.</li><li><b>Resource management issues</b> — inadequate or over-allocated resources delay tasks. Control: proper resource allocation and resource optimization (levelling/smoothing).</li><li><b>Tracking tasks and deadlines</b> — poor tracking leads to missed deadlines. Control: project management software (e.g. MS Project, Jira).</li><li><b>Communication inadequacies</b> — unclear or delayed information causes wrong or late work. Control: a broad communication plan.</li><li><b>Unrealistic deadlines</b> — based on wrong assumptions in estimates. Control: better estimation techniques and schedule compression (crashing, fast tracking).</li></ol>"},{"q":"With examples, explain the four types of logical relationships used in the Precedence Diagramming Method.","pts":["PDM used in Sequence Activities to build network diagram","FS with example (most common)","SS with example","FF with example","SF with example (rare)","Mentions leads/lags (bonus)"],"model":"<p>The <b>Precedence Diagramming Method</b> is used in Sequence Activities to build the project schedule network diagram. For a predecessor A and successor B:</p><ol><li><b>Finish-to-Start</b> — B cannot start until A finishes. Most common. E.g. test the login after coding it.</li><li><b>Start-to-Start</b> — B cannot start until A starts. E.g. writing test cases starts when coding starts.</li><li><b>Finish-to-Finish</b> — B cannot finish until A finishes. E.g. documentation cannot finish until coding finishes.</li><li><b>Start-to-Finish</b> — B cannot finish until A starts. Rare. E.g. the old system cannot be switched off until the new one starts.</li></ol><p>Leads (acceleration) and lags (delay) adjust these relationships.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"What is the duration of a milestone?","o":["One day","Zero","Variable","One week"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"\"B cannot start until A finishes\" is…","o":["SS","FF","FS","SF"],"a":2},{"k":"mcq","q":"The rarest PDM relationship is…","o":["FS","SS","FF","SF"],"a":3},{"k":"gap","q":"Waiting time between activities, such as concrete curing, is a ___.","a":"lag"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Paths: A-D = 15 days, B-E-D = 25, C-F = 35. The critical path is…","o":["A-D","B-E-D","C-F","None"],"a":2},{"k":"tf","q":"The critical path is the shortest path through the network.","a":false,"w":"It is the longest path, giving the shortest possible project duration."},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which estimating technique is most accurate but slowest?","o":["Analogous","Parametric","Bottom-up","Three-point"],"a":2},{"k":"mcq","q":"Overlapping activities normally done in sequence is…","o":["Crashing","Fast tracking","Resource levelling","Rolling wave"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Crashing mainly increases…","o":["Risk","Cost","Scope","Float"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Planning near-term work in detail and later work at a higher level is ___ wave planning.","a":"rolling"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Main output of Sequence Activities?","o":["Activity list","Project schedule network diagram","Schedule baseline","Duration estimates"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a Control Schedule tool?","o":["Iteration burndown chart","Decomposition","Rolling wave planning","Benchmarking"],"a":0}]},{"id":"t9","n":"Topic 9","title":"Project Budgeting & Managing Project Resources","why":"Two halves: the money (estimate → budget) and the resources (types + levelling vs smoothing). Both have a diagram or table on the slides, which makes them likely questions.","cards":[{"h":"Cost management: 4 processes","b":"\n  <p>Project budgeting is carried out under <b>Project Cost Management</b>:</p>\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Plan Cost Mgmt</span><em>→</em><span>Estimate Costs</span><em>→</em><span>Determine Budget</span><em>→</em><span>Control Costs</span></div>\n  <p><b>Plan Cost Management</b> → <b>cost management plan</b>: how costs will be estimated, budgeted, managed, monitored and controlled. Inputs include the charter, schedule management plan and risk management plan.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Estimate = how MUCH each piece costs. Budget = add it all up and get it APPROVED.</div>"},{"h":"Estimate Costs","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Estimate Costs</b>The process of developing an approximation of the monetary resources needed to complete project work.</div>\n  <ul><li>Estimates are <b>quantitative assessments of the probable costs</b> to complete the work.</li>\n  <li>Cover <b>all resources</b>: labour, materials, equipment, services, facilities, plus cost of financing and inflation allowance.</li>\n  <li>Include <b>contingency</b> for identified risks.</li>\n  <li>Are <b>aggregated by work package</b> (from the WBS) → control accounts → whole project.</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> expert judgement, analogous, parametric, three-point, bottom-up estimating, reserve analysis, <b>cost of quality</b>, PMIS, voting.</p>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b> cost estimates + basis of estimates.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">One slide says estimates include management reserve; the budget slide says the cost baseline <b>excludes</b> it. In the exam use the budget rule: <b>Budget = Cost baseline + Management reserve</b>.</div>"},{"h":"Determine Budget","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Determine Budget</b>The process of aggregating the estimated costs of individual activities or work packages to establish an authorized cost baseline, used to monitor and control project performance.</div>\n  <p><b>Outputs:</b></p>\n  <ul><li><b>Cost baseline</b> — the approved version of the <b>time-phased</b> project budget, <b>excluding any management reserves</b>; changed only through formal change control.</li>\n  <li><b>Project funding requirements</b> — forecast costs to be paid, derived from the cost baseline (total or periodic): projected expenditures plus anticipated liabilities.</li></ul>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> cost aggregation, reserve analysis, historical information review, <b>funding limit reconciliation</b>, financing. Inputs include the <b>business case</b> and <b>benefits management plan</b>.</p>\n  <div class=\"trig\">\"time-phased, excluding management reserve\" → cost baseline</div>"},{"h":"Budget components (the stack diagram)","b":"\n  <p>Build it from the bottom up:</p>\n  <div class=\"flow\" style=\"flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch\">\n   <span style=\"background:#5a3c0c\">PROJECT BUDGET = Cost baseline + Management reserve</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#7a5212\">Management reserve (unplanned work — unknown unknowns)</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#8a5a14\">Cost baseline</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#a06c1e\">Control accounts</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#b47f2a\">Work package cost estimates + contingency reserve</span>\n   <span style=\"background:#c99640\">Activity cost estimates + activity contingency reserve</span></div>\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Contingency reserve</th><th>Management reserve</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>For</td><td><b>Identified</b> risks (known unknowns)</td><td><b>Unplanned</b> work (unknown unknowns)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Where</td><td><b>Inside</b> the cost baseline</td><td><b>Outside</b> the baseline, inside the budget</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Control</td><td>PM</td><td>Management approval needed</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Example:</b> work packages ₦60m + contingency ₦6m = cost baseline ₦66m; + management reserve ₦4m = project budget ₦70m.</p>"},{"h":"4 types of project resources","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project resources</b>The essential elements, both tangible and intangible, required to ensure successful completion of projects. Proper allocation, monitoring and control is crucial.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Includes</th><th>If badly managed</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Human</b></td><td>Project team led by the PM; full-time, part-time, specialists</td><td>Limited skilled staff, overallocation, poor training → delays, burnout, poor quality</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Physical</b></td><td>Equipment, materials, facilities, infrastructure</td><td>Missing equipment → delay; poor materials → recalls/rework; too much inventory → high cost; too little → shortages</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Financial</b></td><td>Funds for labour, equipment, materials, overhead</td><td>Insufficient funds → quality issues, delay, cancellation; overspend → budget overrun</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Time</b></td><td>Schedules and deadlines; estimation; tracking</td><td>Unrealistic deadlines, unclear timelines → rushed work, quality issues, unmet expectations</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">H-P-F-T: People, Property, Finance, Time.</div>"},{"h":"Resource levelling","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Resource levelling</b>A technique in which start and finish dates are adjusted based on resource constraints, with the goal of balancing the demand for resources with the available supply.</div>\n  <p>Used when key resources are available only at certain times, in limited quantities, or are <b>over-allocated</b>. It <b>may change the critical path</b> and often <b>extends</b> the project.</p>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's example:</b> A needs John + Jane, B needs Jane, both on Day 1, then C (John) on Day 2. Jane is booked 16 hrs on Day 1 → over-allocated. Levelling delays B to Day 2, C moves to Day 3. Nobody exceeds 8 hrs, but the project goes from <b>2 days to 3 days</b> and the critical path becomes A → B → C.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">LEVELLING: Limited resources → dates move → critical path may change.</div>"},{"h":"Resource smoothing + the comparison","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Resource smoothing</b>A technique that adjusts activities so resource requirements do not exceed predefined limits. It moves activities <b>within their float</b>, without changing the project duration or critical path, to get more <b>uniform</b> resource use.</div>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's example:</b> limit of 6 people on the whole project. Days 3–4 needed 7 (A: 5 + B: 2). B had free float, so its work was spread at 1 person/day over Days 2–6. Peak = 6, end date unchanged.</p>\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Levelling</th><th>Smoothing</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Objective</td><td>Balance demand with supply</td><td>Uniform resource utilization</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Critical path</td><td>Often <b>changes</b></td><td><b>Not changed</b>; uses float</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Main constraint</td><td><b>Critical resources</b></td><td><b>Project duration</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>When</td><td>Resources limited / available only at certain times / over-allocated</td><td>Limits on resource usage or uneven usage</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Dates</td><td>Start and end dates can change</td><td>Meets the initial deadline</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Smoothing needs float. If there's no float, it can't work; you must level (and accept a later end date).</div>"},{"h":"Cost ITTO extras","b":"<table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Extras on the slides</th></tr><tr><td>Plan Cost Mgmt</td><td>Expert judgement, <b>alternative analysis</b>, meetings</td></tr><tr><td>Estimate Costs</td><td>Reserve analysis, <b>cost of quality</b>, alternative analysis, voting; inputs include the quality plan, scope baseline, schedule, team assignments, risk register</td></tr><tr><td>Determine Budget</td><td>Cost aggregation, <b>historical information review</b>, <b>funding limit reconciliation</b>, financing; inputs include the <b>business case</b> and <b>benefits management plan</b></td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Resources: the extra slide points","b":"<ul><li><b>Human</b>: the team works collectively toward a <b>shared goal</b>; experts can be brought in as needed.</li><li><b>Financial</b>: funding problems come from <b>poor budgeting, unforeseen expenses or scope changes</b>. Overspending can threaten the project's <b>viability</b>.</li><li><b>Time</b>: accurate estimation helps allocate resources; <b>real-time tracking</b> finds bottlenecks so resources can be reallocated.</li></ul><p>Constrained resources = insufficient personnel, tight deadlines, tight budget. Levelling and smoothing are both <b>resource optimisation</b> techniques.</p>"},{"h":"The smoothing slide constraints","b":"<p>The smoothing diagram on the slide has two constraints:</p><ol><li>Only <b>5</b> people can work on activity A at any time (<b>scarce resource</b>).</li><li>At most <b>6</b> people on the whole project at any time (<b>predefined resource limit</b>).</li></ol><p>A uses 5 a day; B's 2-person days push the total to 7. B has <b>free float</b>, so it is spread at 1 person a day. The total stays at 6 and the end date does not move.</p>"}],"recall":[["Name the 4 cost management processes.","Plan Cost Management, Estimate Costs, Determine Budget, Control Costs."],["Define Determine Budget.","Aggregating the estimated costs of individual activities or work packages to establish an authorized cost baseline."],["What is the cost baseline?","The approved version of the time-phased project budget, excluding management reserves; changed only through formal change control."],["Project budget formula?","Project Budget = Cost Baseline + Management Reserve."],["Contingency vs management reserve?","Contingency covers identified risks and is inside the cost baseline. Management reserve covers unplanned work and sits outside the baseline (inside the budget)."],["What are project funding requirements?","Forecast costs to be paid, derived from the cost baseline, for total or periodic needs: expenditures plus anticipated liabilities."],["4 types of project resources?","Human, physical, financial, time."],["Define resource levelling.","Adjusting start and finish dates based on resource constraints to balance resource demand with available supply; may change the critical path."],["Define resource smoothing.","Adjusting activities within their float so resource needs stay within predefined limits, without changing the critical path or project end date."],["Which one has project duration as the main constraint?","Smoothing. (Levelling: critical resources are the main constraint.)"],["Name two tools of Determine Budget besides cost aggregation.","Reserve analysis, historical information review, funding limit reconciliation, financing (any two)."],["Which business documents are inputs to Determine Budget?","The business case and the benefits management plan."],["Three causes of funding problems (slides).","Poor budgeting, unforeseen expenses, changes in scope."],["What do levelling and smoothing have in common?","Both are resource optimisation techniques for managing resource constraints."],["How does real-time tracking help manage time as a resource?","It identifies bottlenecks so resources can be reallocated to keep the project on track."]],"theory":[{"q":"With the aid of a diagram, explain the components of a project budget.","pts":["Activity cost estimates + activity contingency","Work package estimates + contingency reserve","Control accounts","Cost baseline (time-phased, excludes management reserve)","Management reserve for unplanned work","Budget = baseline + management reserve","Contingency (identified risks) vs management reserve distinguished","Diagram drawn bottom-up"],"model":"<p>The project budget is built up from the lowest level of cost:</p><ol><li><b>Activity cost estimates</b> plus an <b>activity contingency reserve</b> for identified risks.</li><li>These are aggregated into <b>work package cost estimates</b>, with a further <b>contingency reserve</b>.</li><li>Work packages are grouped into <b>control accounts</b> for management control.</li><li>Control accounts plus contingency make up the <b>cost baseline</b>: the approved, time-phased budget excluding management reserve, changed only through formal change control.</li><li>A <b>management reserve</b> is added for unplanned work (unknown unknowns). It is held by management and released only with approval.</li><li><b>Project budget = cost baseline + management reserve.</b></li></ol><p>[Draw the stack: activity estimates at the bottom → work packages → control accounts → cost baseline → management reserve → project budget at the top.]</p>"},{"q":"Differentiate between resource levelling and resource smoothing.","pts":["Levelling definition (adjust dates, balance demand and supply)","Smoothing definition (within float, uniform use)","Critical path: changes vs unchanged","Constraint: critical resources vs project duration","Dates/duration: may extend vs initial deadline kept","When each is used","Example"],"model":"<table><tr><th>Resource levelling</th><th>Resource smoothing</th></tr><tr><td>Adjusts start and finish dates based on resource constraints to balance demand for resources with available supply.</td><td>Adjusts activities within their float so requirements don't exceed predefined limits, giving uniform utilization.</td></tr><tr><td>Often changes the critical path.</td><td>Does not change the critical path; uses float.</td></tr><tr><td>Critical resources are the primary constraint.</td><td>Project duration is the primary constraint.</td></tr><tr><td>Used when resources are available only at certain times, in limited quantity, or over-allocated.</td><td>Used when there are limits on resource usage or usage is uneven.</td></tr><tr><td>Start and end dates may change (project may be extended).</td><td>Meets the initial deadline.</td></tr></table><p>E.g. levelling: one DBA booked on two parallel tasks → one task delayed, project ends later. Smoothing: a non-critical documentation task spread over its float to avoid a staffing peak.</p>"},{"q":"Explain the four types of project resources and the implications of poorly managing each.","pts":["Definition of project resources","Human + implications","Physical + implications","Financial + implications","Time + implications","Examples given"],"model":"<p><b>Project resources</b> are the tangible and intangible elements required to complete a project successfully.</p><ol><li><b>Human</b> — the team led by the PM. Poor management (limited skilled staff, overallocation, no training) leads to delays, burnout and poor quality.</li><li><b>Physical</b> — equipment, materials, facilities. Failing to secure equipment causes delays; poor materials cause recalls and rework; too much inventory raises costs, too little causes shortages.</li><li><b>Financial</b> — funds for labour, equipment and overheads. Insufficient funds cause quality problems, delay or cancellation; poor budgeting causes overruns.</li><li><b>Time</b> — schedules and deadlines. Unrealistic deadlines and unexpected delays cause rushed work, quality issues and unmet expectations.</li></ol>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"Project budget = cost baseline + …","o":["Contingency reserve","Management reserve","Funding requirements","Control accounts"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"The contingency reserve covers…","o":["Unknown unknowns","Identified risks (known unknowns)","Scope creep only","Inflation only"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"The cost baseline includes the management reserve.","a":false,"w":"The cost baseline excludes management reserves."},{"k":"mcq","q":"\"Time-phased budget, excluding management reserve\" describes the…","o":["Project budget","Cost baseline","Funding requirements","Cost estimate"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which technique may change the critical path?","o":["Resource smoothing","Resource levelling","Both","Neither"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Resource smoothing relies on…","o":["Adding staff","Activity float","Crashing","Extending the end date"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Main constraint in resource smoothing?","o":["Critical resources","Project duration","Budget","Scope"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Ordering low-quality material may cause a high rate of recalls or ___.","a":"rework"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a tool of Estimate Costs?","o":["Cost of quality","Funding limit reconciliation","Decomposition","Inspection"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"Forecast costs to be paid, derived from the cost baseline, are…","o":["Cost estimates","Project funding requirements","Management reserve","Control accounts"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is NOT one of the four types of project resources?","o":["Human","Physical","Financial","Political"],"a":3}]},{"id":"t10","n":"Topic 10","title":"Managing Project Risk","why":"The biggest topic here. Know the definition, the 7 types, the 7 processes, the P×I matrix, EMV, and the 5 response strategies.","cards":[{"h":"What is a risk?","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project risk</b>An uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, can impact a project's objectives such as scope, budget, timeline or quality.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Positive risk (opportunity)</th><th>Negative risk (threat)</th></tr><tr><td>New technology lets you finish early; favourable interest rates</td><td>Supplier delays; key staff resign</td></tr></table>\n  <table><tr><th>Internal</th><th>External</th></tr><tr><td>Communication, resource availability</td><td>Regulatory changes, exchange/interest rates</td></tr></table>\n  <p>Risks vary in <b>likelihood</b> and <b>impact</b>; identify and prioritise them early.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Risk = MAYBE. If it's already happening, it's an <b>issue</b>.</div>"},{"h":"7 types of project risk","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Cause</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Scope</b></td><td>Requirements unclear, change unexpectedly, or expand without authorization (scope creep)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Schedule</b></td><td>Unrealistic timelines, resource shortages, ignoring the critical path, external dependencies</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Cost / financial</b></td><td>Costs exceed budget: poor estimation, unexpected expenses</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Resource</b></td><td>Lack of people, equipment, materials: overallocation, resignations, injuries, supply issues</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Operational</b></td><td>Internal processes, systems, team dynamics: poor communication, inadequate training</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Technical</b></td><td>Technology: bugs, hardware failure, outdated systems, e.g. migrating from servers to cloud → data loss, downtime</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>External</b></td><td>Outside team's control: regulation, market shifts, natural disasters (e.g. rainy season)</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>S</b>ix <b>S</b>mart <b>C</b>ats <b>R</b>un <b>O</b>ver <b>T</b>he <b>E</b>dge\" — Scope, Schedule, Cost, Resource, Operational, Technical, External.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Classify by <b>cause</b>: naira devaluing makes servers dearer → <b>external</b> (cost is just the effect).</div>"},{"h":"Risk management: 7 processes","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project risk management</b>The processes of conducting risk management planning, identification, analysis, response planning, response implementation and monitoring of risk. Objective: increase the probability/impact of positive risks and decrease the probability/impact of negative risks.</div>\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Plan Risk Mgmt</span><em>→</em><span>Identify Risks</span><em>→</em><span>Qualitative Analysis</span><em>→</em><span>Quantitative Analysis</span><em>→</em><span>Plan Responses</span><em>→</em><span>Implement Responses</span><em>→</em><span>Monitor Risks</span></div>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Plan → Find → Rank → Number → Respond → Do → Watch.</div>\n  <p><b>Plan Risk Management</b> — defines how to conduct risk management; makes the effort proportionate to the risks and the project's importance. Output: <b>risk management plan</b>. Tool: stakeholder analysis.</p>\n  <p>Risk management is <b>not a one-time task</b>: it's ongoing and iterative.</p>"},{"h":"Identify Risks: register vs report","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Identify Risks</b>The process of identifying individual project risks as well as sources of overall project risk, and documenting their characteristics.</div>\n  <p><b>Tools:</b> brainstorming, checklists, interviews, root cause analysis, assumption & constraint analysis, <b>SWOT</b>, document analysis, prompt lists, facilitation.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Risk register</th><th>Risk report</th></tr><tr><td>Details of <b>individual</b> risks; potential owners and responses</td><td>Sources of <b>overall</b> project risk + summary (number of threats/opportunities, by category)</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Register columns (lecturer's template):</b> ID · date raised · description · likelihood · impact · severity · owner · mitigating action.</p>\n  <p>E.g. \"Exchange rate depreciation → budget overrun\" · Medium · High · High · Sponsor · contingency reserve + monitor rates.</p>"},{"h":"Qualitative analysis: P × I matrix","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Perform Qualitative Risk Analysis</b>The process of prioritizing individual project risks for further analysis or action by assessing their probability of occurrence and impact.</div>\n  <p><b>Risk score = Probability × Impact</b></p>\n  <table class=\"mono\"><tr><th>P ↓ / I →</th><th>.05</th><th>.10</th><th>.20</th><th>.40</th><th>.80</th></tr>\n  <tr><th>.90</th><td>.05</td><td>.09</td><td>.18</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.36</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.72</td></tr>\n  <tr><th>.70</th><td>.04</td><td>.07</td><td>.14</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.28</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.56</td></tr>\n  <tr><th>.50</th><td>.03</td><td>.05</td><td>.10</td><td>.20</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.40</td></tr>\n  <tr><th>.30</th><td>.02</td><td>.03</td><td>.06</td><td>.12</td><td style=\"background:#f6d4d1\">.24</td></tr>\n  <tr><th>.10</th><td>.01</td><td>.01</td><td>.02</td><td>.04</td><td>.08</td></tr></table>\n  <p>Green ≈ ≤0.07 (low) · Yellow ≈ 0.08–0.20 (medium) · <b>Red ≥ 0.24 (high)</b>.</p>\n  <p>Tools: probability & impact assessment, P&I matrix, risk categorization, risk data quality assessment. Output: <b>updates</b> to the risk register/report.</p>"},{"h":"Quantitative analysis: EMV & decision tree","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Perform Quantitative Risk Analysis</b>Numerically analyzing the combined effect of identified risks and other uncertainty on overall project objectives. <b>Not required for every project.</b></div>\n  <p>Tools: <b>simulation</b>, <b>sensitivity analysis</b>, <b>decision tree analysis</b>, <b>influence diagrams</b>. Output: risk report update.</p>\n  <p><b>EMV = Probability × Impact</b>, summed over each outcome of an option.</p>\n  <p><b>Lecturer's example:</b> late penalty ₦20m. In-house costs ₦4m with 30% chance of default. Outsource costs ₦8m with 5% chance.</p>\n  <table class=\"mono\"><tr><th>Option</th><th>Working</th><th>EMV</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>In-house</td><td>0.30 × −24 + 0.70 × −4 = −7.2 − 2.8</td><td><b>−₦10m</b></td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Outsource</td><td>0.05 × −28 + 0.95 × −8 = −1.4 − 7.6</td><td><b>−₦9m</b></td></tr></table>\n  <p>Choose <b>outsource</b> (lower expected cost), even though it's dearer upfront.</p>\n  <table><tr><th>Qualitative</th><th>Quantitative</th></tr><tr><td>Prioritizes individual risks</td><td>Numerically measures overall exposure</td></tr><tr><td>Ratings (low/med/high)</td><td>Money, time, probabilities</td></tr><tr><td>Normally always done</td><td>Not required for every project</td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Plan, Implement, Monitor + RBS","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Key tools/outputs</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Plan Risk Responses</b></td><td>Developing options, selecting strategies and agreeing actions for overall and individual risks</td><td>Strategies for threats/opportunities, contingent responses, cost-benefit analysis</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Implement Risk Responses</b></td><td>Implementing the agreed risk response plans</td><td>Influencing, PMIS</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Monitor Risks</b></td><td>Monitoring responses, tracking risks, identifying new ones, evaluating effectiveness</td><td>Technical performance analysis, reserve analysis, audits → WPI, change requests</td></tr></table>\n  <h3>Risk Breakdown Structure (RBS)</h3>\n  <p>A hierarchical representation of potential <b>sources</b> of risk. Level 1 categories:</p>\n  <p><span class=\"pill\">1 Technical</span><span class=\"pill\">2 Management</span><span class=\"pill\">3 Commercial</span><span class=\"pill\">4 External</span></p>\n  <p>E.g. Technical: scope definition, requirements, technology · Management: PM, resourcing, communication · Commercial: contracts, suppliers, customer stability · External: legislation, <b>exchange rates</b>, weather, competition.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">WBS breaks down <b>work</b>. RBS (risk) breaks down <b>sources of risk</b>.</div>"},{"h":"5 risk response strategies (threats)","b":"\n  <table><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Avoid</b></td><td><b>Eliminate</b> the threat or protect the project from it, e.g. by changing the plan/scope</td><td>Use proven technology instead of a new one; extend schedule; reduce scope</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Mitigate</b></td><td><b>Reduce</b> likelihood or impact; early action beats repair</td><td>More stable supplier; more tests; regular backups</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Accept</b></td><td>Acknowledge, no proactive action. <b>Passive</b> (do nothing) or <b>active</b> (contingency reserve of time/money)</td><td>Extra time for weather delays</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Transfer</b></td><td>Shift ownership/impact to a <b>third party</b>, usually for a <b>risk premium</b></td><td>Insurance for shipped items</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Escalate</b></td><td>Threat is <b>outside project scope</b> or beyond the <b>PM's authority</b> → passed to sponsor/senior management</td><td>Technical hurdle risking reputation and the whole budget</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>A</b>ll <b>M</b>y <b>A</b>nswers <b>T</b>ake <b>E</b>ffort\": Avoid, Mitigate, Accept, Transfer, Escalate.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Avoid = risk can't happen. Mitigate = risk still possible but smaller. Transfer = third party outside; Escalate = up the chain inside the organization.</div>"},{"h":"Risk definitions in full","b":"<p>Risks are <b>inherent in all projects</b>, but complexity varies with size and nature.</p><ul><li><b>Plan Risk Management</b> makes the <b>degree, type and visibility</b> of risk management proportionate to the risks and the project's importance.</li><li><b>Risk management plan</b>: describes how risk activities will be structured and performed.</li><li><b>Risk register</b>: a repository of risk process outputs, updated by the other risk processes.</li><li><b>Quantitative analysis</b> quantifies <b>overall risk exposure</b>. Where used, it runs <b>throughout</b> the project.</li><li><b>Implement Risk Responses</b> ensures agreed responses are executed as planned.</li><li><b>Monitor Risks</b> lets decisions use <b>current information</b>; tools include <b>audits</b>.</li></ul>"},{"h":"Risk register: the template rows","b":"<table><tr><th>Risk</th><th>L / I / S</th><th>Owner</th><th>Action</th></tr><tr><td>Delay in critical materials</td><td>M / H / H</td><td>PM</td><td>Avoid a single vendor; late-delivery penalty</td></tr><tr><td>Incomplete design and deliverables</td><td>L / H / H</td><td>PM</td><td>Design review workshop with experts</td></tr><tr><td>Exchange rate depreciation</td><td>M / H / H</td><td>Sponsor</td><td>Contingency reserve; monitor rates</td></tr><tr><td>Scope creep</td><td>L / M / M</td><td>PM</td><td>Strict change control</td></tr><tr><td>Contractor delays</td><td>M / H / H</td><td>PM</td><td>Communicate schedule early; delay penalty in contracts</td></tr></table><p>L = likelihood, I = impact, S = severity.</p>"},{"h":"Decision tree: how to read it","b":"<p>A decision tree helps choose the best of several courses of action. <b>Branches</b> are decisions or events, each with costs and risks.</p><p>Compute the EMV of each option. Choose the <b>lower expected cost</b> or the <b>higher expected benefit</b>.</p><p>Lecture tree: in-house (−₦4m) → delay 30% (−₦24m) or none 70% (−₦4m); outsource (−₦8m) → delay 5% (−₦28m) or none 95% (−₦8m). Each delay outcome adds the ₦20m penalty to the option's cost.</p>"},{"h":"RBS level 2 and P×I matrix","b":"<table><tr><th>Level 1</th><th>Level 2 examples</th></tr><tr><td>Technical</td><td>Scope, requirements, estimates and assumptions, technical processes, technology, interfaces</td></tr><tr><td>Management</td><td>PM, program/portfolio, operations, organisation, resourcing, communication</td></tr><tr><td>Commercial</td><td>Contract terms, internal procurement, suppliers, subcontracts, client stability, partnerships</td></tr><tr><td>External</td><td>Legislation, exchange rates, site, weather, competition, regulatory</td></tr></table><p>An organisation can use a <b>generic</b> RBS or tailor one. The <b>P×I matrix</b> maps each risk's probability and impact on schedule, cost, quality and performance to give a <b>priority level</b>.</p>"},{"h":"Response strategy fine print","b":"<ul><li><b>Avoid</b>: remove the cause of the threat, or remove the tasks that contain it.</li><li><b>Mitigate</b>: also adopt <b>less complex processes</b>; backups and cloud storage reduce data-loss impact.</li><li><b>Accept</b>: suits <b>low-priority</b> threats, or when no other response is possible or cost-effective.</li><li><b>Escalate</b>: to the sponsor, a director or another senior stakeholder.</li></ul><p>Plan Risk Responses also covers <b>strategies for opportunities</b> and for <b>overall project risk</b>, using alternative analysis, cost-benefit analysis and multicriteria decision analysis.</p>"}],"recall":[["Define project risk.","An uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, can impact a project's objectives such as scope, budget, timeline or quality. It can be positive (opportunity) or negative (threat)."],["Name the 7 types of project risk.","Scope, schedule, cost/financial, resource, operational, technical, external."],["Name the 7 risk management processes.","Plan Risk Management, Identify Risks, Perform Qualitative Risk Analysis, Perform Quantitative Risk Analysis, Plan Risk Responses, Implement Risk Responses, Monitor Risks."],["Risk register vs risk report?","Register: details of individual risks, owners and responses. Report: sources of overall project risk plus summary information."],["P = 0.7, I = 0.4. Score and band?","0.28 → high (red)."],["EMV formula?","EMV = Probability × Impact, summed over all outcomes of an option."],["4 quantitative analysis tools?","Simulation, sensitivity analysis, decision tree analysis, influence diagrams."],["Is quantitative analysis required for every project?","No."],["5 response strategies for threats?","Avoid, mitigate, accept, transfer, escalate."],["Passive vs active acceptance?","Passive: do nothing. Active: set aside a contingency reserve of time, money or resources."],["4 level-1 categories of the RBS?","Technical, management, commercial, external."],["Insurance is which strategy?","Transfer (to a third party, with a premium)."],["What does Plan Risk Management make proportionate?","The degree, type and visibility of risk management, proportionate to the risks and the project's importance."],["Define the risk management plan.","A component of the PM plan describing how risk management activities will be structured and performed."],["When is quantitative risk analysis performed, if used?","Throughout the project."],["In a decision tree, which option do you choose?","The one with the lower expected cost or higher expected benefit."],["Name two level-2 items under commercial risk in the RBS.","Contract terms, internal procurement, suppliers and vendors, subcontracts, client stability, partnerships (any two)."],["When is risk acceptance suitable?","For low-priority threats, or when no other response is possible or cost-effective."],["Name the two tools of Monitor Risks besides meetings.","Data analysis (technical performance analysis, reserve analysis) and audits."],["Mitigating action for \"delay in critical materials\" in the template?","Avoid using one vendor for critical supplies; include a penalty for late delivery."]],"theory":[{"q":"Define project risk and explain any five types of risk in projects, with examples.","pts":["Definition (uncertain event, if it occurs, impacts objectives)","Positive vs negative mentioned","Type 1 explained + example","Type 2 explained + example","Type 3 explained + example","Type 4 explained + example","Type 5 explained + example"],"model":"<p>A <b>project risk</b> is an uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, can impact a project's objectives such as scope, budget, timeline or quality. Risks may be negative (threats) or positive (opportunities).</p><ol><li><b>Scope risk</b> — requirements are unclear, change unexpectedly or expand without formal authorization (scope creep). E.g. a client keeps adding features.</li><li><b>Schedule risk</b> — the project may miss its planned schedule due to unrealistic timelines, resource shortages or ignoring the critical path.</li><li><b>Cost risk</b> — actual costs may exceed the budget due to poor estimation or unexpected expenses.</li><li><b>Resource risk</b> — the project may lack personnel, equipment or materials, e.g. a key developer resigns.</li><li><b>Technical risk</b> — technology problems such as software bugs or hardware failure, e.g. data loss when migrating from local servers to the cloud.</li><li>(Also operational: poor internal processes/communication; external: regulation, market shifts, weather.)</li></ol>"},{"q":"Explain the five risk response strategies for negative risks, with an example of each.","pts":["Avoid: eliminate/change plan + example","Mitigate: reduce probability/impact + example","Accept: passive vs active (contingency) + example","Transfer: third party, premium + example","Escalate: outside scope/authority → sponsor + example"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Avoidance</b> — acting to eliminate the threat or protect the project from its impact, e.g. by changing the plan or scope. E.g. using a proven technology instead of an untested one.</li><li><b>Mitigation</b> — reducing the probability or impact of the threat. E.g. choosing a more reliable supplier, adding more tests, keeping regular backups.</li><li><b>Acceptance</b> — acknowledging the threat without proactive action. Passive acceptance does nothing; active acceptance sets aside a contingency reserve. E.g. adding extra time for rainy-season delays.</li><li><b>Transfer</b> — shifting ownership and impact of the threat to a third party, usually for a risk premium. E.g. insuring equipment being shipped.</li><li><b>Escalation</b> — used when the threat is outside the project scope or beyond the PM's authority; it is passed to the sponsor or senior management. E.g. a technical hurdle that could cause reputational damage to the whole organization.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Differentiate between qualitative and quantitative risk analysis. Use an example to show how expected monetary value (EMV) supports a decision.","pts":["Qualitative: prioritizes individual risks by probability and impact","Qualitative tool: P&I matrix","Quantitative: numerically analyzes combined effect / overall exposure","Quantitative tools (simulation, sensitivity, decision tree, influence diagrams); not always required","EMV = P × I","Correct EMV calculation for two options","Correct decision stated"],"model":"<p><b>Qualitative risk analysis</b> prioritizes individual risks for further action by assessing probability and impact, using descriptive ratings and the probability-impact matrix (score = P × I). It is quick and normally done on all projects.</p><p><b>Quantitative risk analysis</b> numerically analyzes the combined effect of risks on overall project objectives, using simulation, sensitivity analysis, decision trees and influence diagrams. It is not required for every project.</p><p><b>EMV example:</b> a late-delivery penalty is ₦20m. In-house: cost ₦4m, 30% chance of delay. EMV = 0.3 × (−24) + 0.7 × (−4) = −₦10m. Outsource: cost ₦8m, 5% chance. EMV = 0.05 × (−28) + 0.95 × (−8) = −₦9m. Outsourcing has the lower expected cost, so it is chosen.</p>"},{"q":"Briefly explain the seven project risk management processes.","pts":["Plan Risk Management → risk management plan","Identify Risks → risk register + risk report","Qualitative → prioritize by P & I","Quantitative → numerical, overall exposure","Plan Risk Responses → strategies/actions","Implement Risk Responses → carry out plans","Monitor Risks → track, new risks, effectiveness","Ongoing/iterative"],"model":"<ol><li><b>Plan Risk Management</b> — defines how risk management will be conducted; output: risk management plan.</li><li><b>Identify Risks</b> — identifies individual risks and sources of overall risk; outputs: risk register and risk report.</li><li><b>Perform Qualitative Risk Analysis</b> — prioritizes risks by probability and impact.</li><li><b>Perform Quantitative Risk Analysis</b> — numerically analyzes the combined effect of risks (simulation, decision trees).</li><li><b>Plan Risk Responses</b> — develops options, selects strategies and agrees actions.</li><li><b>Implement Risk Responses</b> — carries out the agreed response plans.</li><li><b>Monitor Risks</b> — monitors responses, tracks risks, identifies new ones and evaluates effectiveness.</li></ol><p>Risk management is ongoing and iterative, not a one-time task.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"A risk that is already happening is…","o":["A threat","An issue","An opportunity","A residual risk"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"A new technology that lets the project finish early is…","o":["A negative risk","A positive risk (opportunity)","An issue","An assumption"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Migrating from servers to the cloud risks data loss. Risk type?","o":["Scope","Technical","External","Operational"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Working during the rainy season is which risk type?","o":["External","Operational","Resource","Schedule"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"Buying insurance for shipped items is…","o":["Avoid","Mitigate","Transfer","Escalate"],"a":2},{"k":"mcq","q":"Choosing a proven technology instead of a new one is…","o":["Avoid","Accept","Transfer","Mitigate"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"A threat beyond the PM's authority goes to the sponsor. Strategy?","o":["Transfer","Escalate","Accept","Avoid"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Setting aside extra time for weather delays is ___ acceptance.","a":"active"},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which document gives sources of overall project risk and summary information?","o":["Risk register","Risk report","Risk management plan","RBS"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a quantitative risk analysis tool?","o":["P×I matrix","Sensitivity analysis","Risk categorisation","SWOT"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"Quantitative risk analysis is required for every project.","a":false,"w":"It is not required for every project."},{"k":"gap","q":"EMV = probability × ___.","a":"impact"},{"k":"mcq","q":"P = 0.9, I = 0.4. Risk score?","o":["0.36","1.3","0.5","0.04"],"a":0},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a Level-1 RBS category?","o":["Commercial","Budget","Quality","Testing"],"a":0}]},{"id":"t11","n":"Topic 11","title":"Managing Project Quality","why":"Three processes, the Manage-vs-Control distinction, cost of quality, and four tools (fishbone, Pareto, benchmarking/checklists, Six Sigma).","cards":[{"h":"What is quality?","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Quality (PMBOK)</b>The degree to which a set of inherent characteristics fulfils requirements.</div>\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project quality</b>The degree to which a project's deliverables and outcomes meet stakeholder requirements and expectations.</div>\n  <p>Quality is measured against <b>requirements</b>, not luxury. A simple app that does exactly what was asked with no bugs = high quality. A feature-packed app that crashes = low quality.</p>\n  <p>Quality sits in the <b>centre of the triple constraint</b>: squeezing scope, time or cost affects quality.</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Quality = fit to requirements, not fancy.</div>"},{"h":"Quality management: 3 processes","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Project Quality Management</b>The processes for incorporating the organization's quality policy regarding planning, managing and controlling project and product quality requirements, in order to meet stakeholder objectives.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Group</th><th>Outputs</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Plan Quality Management</b> — identify quality requirements/standards and document how compliance will be demonstrated</td><td>Planning</td><td>Quality management plan; quality metrics</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Manage Quality</b> — translate the quality plan into executable quality activities</td><td>Executing</td><td>Quality reports; test & evaluation documents; change requests</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Control Quality</b> — monitor and record results to ensure outputs are complete, correct and meet customer expectations</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling</td><td>Quality control measurements; <b>verified deliverables</b>; work performance information; change requests</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">P-M-C: <b>P</b>lan the standard, <b>M</b>anage the process, <b>C</b>ontrol the product.</div>"},{"h":"Plan Quality: plan vs metrics","b":"\n  <p><b>Quality management plan</b> — part of the PM plan; describes how policies, procedures and guidelines will be implemented to achieve quality objectives. (The HOW.)</p>\n  <p><b>Quality metric</b> — describes a project or product attribute and how Control Quality will verify compliance. (The WHAT you measure.) E.g. errors per line of code, failure rate, page loads in under 2 seconds.</p>\n  <p>Tools include <b>benchmarking</b>, cost-benefit analysis, <b>cost of quality</b>, brainstorming, interviews.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Change requests are NOT an output of Plan Quality Management.</div>"},{"h":"Manage Quality vs Control Quality","b":"\n  <table><tr><th></th><th>Manage Quality (QA)</th><th>Control Quality (QC)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Focus</td><td><b>Process</b> — are we following the right procedures? Can they improve?</td><td><b>Product</b> — is this deliverable complete and correct?</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Group</td><td>Executing</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Tools</td><td>Audits, process analysis, root cause analysis, quality improvement methods</td><td>Inspection, testing, checklists, check sheets, statistical sampling</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Outputs</td><td>Quality reports, test & evaluation documents</td><td>QC measurements, verified deliverables</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Quality report:</b> quality issues, recommendations for corrective action, summary of QC findings.</p>\n  <h3>Deliverable journey</h3>\n  <div class=\"flow\"><span>Deliverables</span><em>→ Control Quality →</em><span>Verified (correct)</span><em>→ Validate Scope →</em><span>Accepted (customer signs)</span></div>\n  <div class=\"hook\">Bakery: Plan = set the recipe standard. Manage = make sure bakers follow it and audit the kitchen. Control = weigh and inspect each loaf. Validate Scope = customer accepts.</div>"},{"h":"Cost of Quality","b":"\n  <table><tr><th colspan=\"2\">Cost of Conformance (spent to AVOID failure)</th><th colspan=\"2\">Cost of Nonconformance (spent BECAUSE of failure)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>Prevention</b><br>\"Build a quality product\"</td><td><b>Appraisal</b><br>\"Assess the quality\"</td><td><b>Internal failure</b><br>\"Found by the project\"</td><td><b>External failure</b><br>\"Found by the customer\"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Training, document processes, equipment, time to do it right</td><td>Testing, destructive testing loss, inspections</td><td>Rework, scrap</td><td>Liabilities, warranty work, lost business</td></tr></table>\n  <p><b>Quick classify:</b> Was there a defect? No → conformance (prevention if stopping defects, appraisal if checking for them). Yes → nonconformance (internal if the team found it, external if the customer did).</p>\n  <div class=\"hook\">P-A-I-E: Prevent, Assess, Inside failure, External failure.</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Testing is an <b>appraisal</b> cost = conformance, even though it finds defects.</div>"},{"h":"Fishbone, Pareto, benchmarking, checklists","b":"\n  <p><b>Cause-and-effect (fishbone / Ishikawa) diagram</b> — a decomposition technique that traces an undesirable effect back to its <b>root cause</b>. Lecturer's example effect: \"Product quality not matching requirements\". Bones: <b>People</b> (fatigue, lack of training), <b>Process</b> (not enough R&D), <b>Equipment</b> (low maintenance), <b>Material</b> (low-quality raw material), <b>Environment</b> (bad working conditions), <b>Management</b> (low commitment to quality).</p>\n  <p><b>Pareto chart</b> — used in quality control to show the <b>most frequently occurring defects by category</b>; bars highest first (80/20 rule) → fix the vital few first.</p>\n  <p><b>Benchmarking</b> — comparing actual or planned products, processes and practices with those of <b>comparable organizations</b> to identify best practices and improvement ideas.</p>\n  <p><b>Checklists</b> — a list of items, actions or points to be considered; helps carry out quality control in a structured way.</p>\n  <div class=\"trap\">Fishbone = WHY (causes). Pareto = HOW OFTEN (frequency).</div>"},{"h":"Six Sigma & DMAIC","b":"\n  <div class=\"def\"><b>Six Sigma</b>A quality control methodology used to eliminate waste and defects in products and services and improve processes, using a step-by-step approach called DMAIC. Achieved when long-term defects are below <b>3.4 defects per million opportunities</b>.</div>\n  <table><tr><th>Step</th><th>What happens</th><th>Example (failed payments)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>D</b>efine</td><td>Choose a process; define the problem</td><td>\"Too many failed online payments\"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>M</b>easure</td><td>Measure the current process; collect data (benchmark); list inputs hindering performance</td><td>4% of payments fail</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>A</b>nalyze</td><td>Isolate and test each input as the possible root cause</td><td>Gateway timeouts at peak hours</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>I</b>mprove</td><td>Implement changes to improve performance</td><td>Add retry + second gateway</td></tr>\n  <tr><td><b>C</b>ontrol</td><td>Add controls so the process doesn't regress</td><td>Monitoring dashboard + alerts</td></tr></table>\n  <div class=\"hook\">\"<b>D</b>on't <b>M</b>ake <b>A</b>ny <b>I</b>ncorrect <b>C</b>hanges.\"</div>\n  <div class=\"trap\">3.4 per MILLION, not 3.4%.</div>"},{"h":"Quality outputs defined","b":"<ul><li><b>Test and evaluation documents</b> (Manage Quality): describe the activities used to check whether the product meets the quality objectives.</li><li><b>Quality control measurements</b> (Control Quality): the documented results of QC activities.</li><li><b>WPI</b> (Control Quality): requirements fulfilment, causes of rejection, rework needed, verified deliverables, need for process adjustments.</li></ul><p>Manage Quality uses <b>QC data</b> to report the overall quality status to stakeholders.</p>"},{"h":"Quality tools by process (slides)","b":"<table><tr><th>Process</th><th>Extra T&T on the slides</th></tr><tr><td>Plan Quality</td><td>Benchmarking, cost-benefit, cost of quality, MCDA, <b>flowcharts</b>, <b>matrix diagrams</b>, mind mapping, <b>test and inspection planning</b></td></tr><tr><td>Manage Quality</td><td>Checklists, document and process analysis, root cause analysis, affinity and <b>cause-and-effect</b> diagrams, flowcharts, <b>audits</b>, <b>problem solving</b>, quality improvement methods</td></tr><tr><td>Control Quality</td><td>Checklists, check sheets, <b>statistical sampling</b>, questionnaires, performance reviews, inspection, testing, <b>control charts</b>, <b>histograms</b>, <b>scatter diagrams</b></td></tr></table>"},{"h":"Cost of quality: the slide definitions","b":"<ul><li><b>Prevention</b>: preventing poor quality in the project's products or services.</li><li><b>Appraisal</b>: evaluating, measuring, <b>auditing</b> and testing them.</li><li><b>Failure</b> (internal or external): non-conformance to stakeholder needs or expectations.</li></ul><p>More fishbone causes on the slide: <b>improper handling</b> and <b>delay in arrival</b> (material), <b>suboptimal manufacturing methods</b> (process).</p><p>Benchmarking also provides a <b>basis for measuring performance</b>.</p>"},{"h":"Quality: the conclusion slide","b":"<ol><li>Quality management applies to <b>all</b> projects, whatever the deliverables.</li><li>Quality measures and techniques are <b>specific to the type of deliverable</b>.</li></ol><p>In DMAIC Analyse, data is used to establish <b>cause and effect</b> and find the root cause of the defect.</p>"}],"recall":[["Define quality (PMBOK).","The degree to which a set of inherent characteristics fulfils requirements."],["3 quality management processes + their groups?","Plan Quality Management (Planning), Manage Quality (Executing), Control Quality (Monitoring & Controlling)."],["2 outputs of Plan Quality Management?","Quality management plan and quality metrics."],["Manage Quality focuses on ___; Control Quality focuses on ___.","Process; product."],["Which process produces verified deliverables? Where do they go next?","Control Quality. They go to Validate Scope, which produces accepted deliverables."],["4 categories of cost of quality?","Prevention and appraisal (conformance); internal failure and external failure (nonconformance)."],["Warranty work is which cost?","External failure (nonconformance)."],["What does a Pareto chart show?","The most frequently occurring defects by category."],["What does a fishbone diagram do?","Traces an undesirable effect back to its root cause; also called cause-and-effect or Ishikawa diagram."],["DMAIC stands for?","Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control."],["Six Sigma defect level?","Fewer than 3.4 defects per million opportunities."],["Define benchmarking.","Comparing actual or planned products, processes and practices with those of comparable organizations to identify best practices and ideas for improvement."],["What are test and evaluation documents?","Documents describing the activities used to determine whether the product meets the quality objectives in the quality plan (output of Manage Quality)."],["What are quality control measurements?","The documented results of Control Quality activities."],["Name three data representation tools in Control Quality.","Cause-and-effect diagrams, control charts, histograms, scatter diagrams (any three)."],["Which process uses audits?","Manage Quality."],["Define appraisal costs.","Costs of evaluating, measuring, auditing and testing the project's products, deliverables or services."],["Two points from the quality conclusion slide.","Quality management applies to all projects; quality measures are specific to the type of deliverable."]],"theory":[{"q":"Define quality and explain the three project quality management processes.","pts":["Quality definition (degree, inherent characteristics, fulfils requirements)","Plan Quality Mgmt: definition + plan & metrics","Manage Quality: definition + reports, test & evaluation docs","Control Quality: definition + QC measurements, verified deliverables","Process groups mentioned","Links verified → Validate Scope"],"model":"<p><b>Quality</b> is the degree to which a set of inherent characteristics fulfils requirements. Project Quality Management covers planning, managing and controlling project and product quality requirements.</p><ol><li><b>Plan Quality Management</b> (Planning) — identifies quality requirements and standards and documents how compliance will be demonstrated. Outputs: quality management plan and quality metrics.</li><li><b>Manage Quality</b> (Executing) — translates the quality management plan into executable quality activities that apply the organization's quality policies. Focuses on the process; uses audits. Outputs: quality reports, test and evaluation documents, change requests.</li><li><b>Control Quality</b> (Monitoring & Controlling) — monitors and records results of quality activities to ensure outputs are complete, correct and meet customer expectations. Focuses on the product; uses inspection and testing. Outputs: quality control measurements, verified deliverables, work performance information, change requests.</li></ol><p>Verified deliverables then go to Validate Scope for formal acceptance.</p>"},{"q":"Explain the cost of quality, giving examples of each category.","pts":["CoQ = conformance + nonconformance","Prevention + examples","Appraisal + examples","Internal failure + examples (found by project)","External failure + examples (found by customer)","Insight: prevention cheaper than failure"],"model":"<p><b>Cost of quality</b> is the total cost of conformance (money spent to avoid failures) and nonconformance (money spent because of failures).</p><p><b>Cost of conformance:</b></p><ul><li><b>Prevention costs</b> — building a quality product: training, documenting processes, equipment, time to do it right.</li><li><b>Appraisal costs</b> — assessing quality: testing, destructive testing loss, inspections.</li></ul><p><b>Cost of nonconformance:</b></p><ul><li><b>Internal failure costs</b> — failures found by the project: rework, scrap.</li><li><b>External failure costs</b> — failures found by the customer: liabilities, warranty work, lost business.</li></ul><p>Spending on prevention and appraisal reduces the much higher cost of failures, especially external ones that damage reputation.</p>"},{"q":"Describe Six Sigma and the DMAIC methodology.","pts":["Six Sigma definition (eliminate waste and defects, improve processes)","3.4 defects per million opportunities","Define explained","Measure explained","Analyze explained","Improve explained","Control explained"],"model":"<p><b>Six Sigma</b> is a quality control methodology used to eliminate waste and defects and improve processes. Six Sigma quality is reached when long-term defects are below 3.4 per million opportunities. It uses <b>DMAIC</b>:</p><ol><li><b>Define</b> — choose a process and define the problem to solve.</li><li><b>Measure</b> — measure key aspects of the current process, collect data as a benchmark, and list inputs that may be hindering performance.</li><li><b>Analyze</b> — isolate each input and test it as a possible root cause; establish cause and effect.</li><li><b>Improve</b> — implement changes that improve performance.</li><li><b>Control</b> — add controls so the process does not regress.</li></ol>"},{"q":"Distinguish between Manage Quality and Control Quality.","pts":["Manage Quality definition","Control Quality definition","Process focus vs product focus","Executing vs Monitoring & Controlling","Outputs of each","Tools: audits vs inspection/testing","Two-way link (QC measurements → MQ; T&E documents → CQ)"],"model":"<table><tr><th>Manage Quality</th><th>Control Quality</th></tr><tr><td>Translates the quality management plan into executable quality activities.</td><td>Monitors and records results of quality activities to make sure outputs are complete, correct and meet expectations.</td></tr><tr><td>Process-focused (quality assurance).</td><td>Product-focused (quality control).</td></tr><tr><td>Executing process group.</td><td>Monitoring & Controlling process group.</td></tr><tr><td>Tools: audits, process analysis, root cause analysis.</td><td>Tools: inspection, testing, checklists, check sheets.</td></tr><tr><td>Outputs: quality reports, test and evaluation documents.</td><td>Outputs: QC measurements, verified deliverables.</td></tr></table><p>They feed each other: Manage Quality uses Control Quality's measurements; Control Quality uses Manage Quality's test and evaluation documents.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"k":"mcq","q":"Quality (PMBOK) is the degree to which a set of inherent characteristics…","o":["Exceeds expectations","Fulfils requirements","Uses premium materials","Satisfies the PM"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Manage Quality belongs to which process group?","o":["Planning","Executing","Monitoring and Controlling","Closing"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which process produces verified deliverables?","o":["Manage Quality","Control Quality","Validate Scope","Plan Quality"],"a":1},{"k":"tf","q":"Change requests are an output of Plan Quality Management.","a":false},{"k":"mcq","q":"Testing is which cost of quality?","o":["Prevention","Appraisal","Internal failure","External failure"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Warranty work is…","o":["Prevention","Appraisal","Internal failure","External failure"],"a":3},{"k":"mcq","q":"Rework found by the team before delivery is…","o":["Appraisal","Internal failure","External failure","Prevention"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which chart shows the most frequent defects by category?","o":["Fishbone","Pareto","Control chart","Scatter diagram"],"a":1},{"k":"gap","q":"Six Sigma quality means fewer than 3.4 defects per ___ opportunities.","a":"million"},{"k":"gap","q":"DMAIC: Define, Measure, Analyse, Improve, ___.","a":"control"},{"k":"mcq","q":"The fishbone diagram is also called…","o":["Pareto chart","Ishikawa diagram","Control chart","Affinity diagram"],"a":1},{"k":"mcq","q":"Which is a Control Quality tool?","o":["Statistical sampling","Audits","Benchmarking","Test and inspection planning"],"a":0},{"k":"tf","q":"Training staff is a cost of conformance.","a":true,"w":"Training is a prevention cost."}]}],"calc":[{"t":"CPM","q":"Activities: A 3 days (start) · B 4 days (after A) · C 2 days (after A) · D 5 days (after B) · E 3 days (after C) · F 2 days (after D and E).<br><b>(a)</b> What is the project duration in days?","ans":14,"tol":0.01,"sol":"Paths: A–B–D–F = 3+4+5+2 = <b>14</b>; A–C–E–F = 3+2+3+2 = 10. Longest = 14 days → critical path A–B–D–F."},{"t":"CPM","q":"Same network as above. <b>(b)</b> How many days of float does activity C have?","ans":4,"tol":0.01,"sol":"C is on the non-critical path A–C–E–F (10 days). Float = 14 − 10 = <b>4 days</b>."},{"t":"PERT","q":"An activity has O = 4, M = 6, P = 14 days. What is its PERT expected duration?","ans":7,"tol":0.01,"sol":"E = (4 + 4×6 + 14) / 6 = 42 / 6 = <b>7 days</b>."},{"t":"PERT","q":"O = 3, M = 5, P = 10. Expected duration? (1 decimal place)","ans":5.5,"tol":0.06,"sol":"E = (3 + 20 + 10) / 6 = 33 / 6 = <b>5.5 days</b>."},{"t":"EMV","q":"Option X costs ₦5m with a 20% chance of a ₦15m penalty. What is the EMV of Option X in ₦m? (type the number; sign optional)","ans":-8,"tol":0.01,"abs":true,"sol":"Delay: 0.2 × −(5+15) = −4. No delay: 0.8 × −5 = −4. EMV = <b>−₦8m</b>."},{"t":"EMV","q":"Option Y costs ₦7m with a 5% chance of the same ₦15m penalty. EMV of Y in ₦m? (2 decimals)","ans":-7.75,"tol":0.01,"abs":true,"sol":"0.05 × −22 = −1.1; 0.95 × −7 = −6.65. EMV = <b>−₦7.75m</b>. Y has the lower expected cost, so choose Y over X (−8)."},{"t":"Budget","q":"Work package estimates total ₦40m. Contingency reserve ₦5m. Management reserve ₦3m. What is the cost baseline in ₦m?","ans":45,"tol":0.01,"sol":"Cost baseline = estimates + contingency = 40 + 5 = <b>₦45m</b> (management reserve excluded)."},{"t":"Budget","q":"Same figures. What is the project budget in ₦m?","ans":48,"tol":0.01,"sol":"Budget = cost baseline + management reserve = 45 + 3 = <b>₦48m</b>."},{"t":"P×I","q":"Probability 0.5, impact 0.4. What is the risk score?","ans":0.2,"tol":0.001,"sol":"0.5 × 0.4 = <b>0.20</b> → medium (yellow) on the lecturer's matrix."},{"t":"P×I","q":"Probability 0.3, impact 0.8. Risk score?","ans":0.24,"tol":0.001,"sol":"0.3 × 0.8 = <b>0.24</b> → high (red). Low chance but very high impact still ranks as high risk."},{"t":"CPM","q":"Paths: A(5)→D(10); B(8)→E(7)→D(10); C(15)→F(20). Project duration in days?","ans":35,"tol":0.01,"sol":"A-D = 15; B-E-D = 25; C-F = <b>35</b> (critical path)."},{"t":"EMV","abs":true,"q":"Lecture: in-house costs ₦4m, 30% chance of a ₦20m late penalty. EMV in ₦m? (sign optional)","ans":-10,"tol":0.01,"sol":"0.3 × (−24) + 0.7 × (−4) = −7.2 − 2.8 = <b>−₦10m</b>."},{"t":"EMV","abs":true,"q":"Lecture: outsource costs ₦8m, 5% chance of the ₦20m penalty. EMV in ₦m? (sign optional)","ans":-9,"tol":0.01,"sol":"0.05 × (−28) + 0.95 × (−8) = −1.4 − 7.6 = <b>−₦9m</b>. Lower expected cost, so outsource."},{"t":"PERT","q":"Lecture school project: B purchase items, O = 3, M = 4, P = 6. Expected time (1 decimal)?","ans":4.2,"tol":0.05,"sol":"(3 + 16 + 6) ÷ 6 = 25 ÷ 6 = <b>4.2</b> days."}]};
